/* generated by tools/build-logic.mjs — edit data/logic/*.json instead */
window.LOGIC = {"se":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","hook":"One mason builds a house from memory; forty flats need drawings that outlive him.","why":"A single program fits in one head. A real system does not: it runs for a decade, changes hands, connects to systems nobody documented, and is edited every month. Without discipline, each change costs more than the last until the team quietly gives up and starts again from scratch.","story":"Take a mason building a house in Kathmandu: for one storey he keeps every measurement in his head — where the pipe runs, which wall is load-bearing. Now give him a forty-flat block and the same trick fails on the third floor, because no single head holds it. The drawings, the bill of quantities and the municipal inspection register are what let strangers continue his work. He also leaves an access panel behind the tap, because a pipe sealed in a wall is cheap today and ruinous later — that is maintainability. He weighs cement against strength, since saving a bag now cracks the slab — that is the quality trade. And the flat must join a sewer of unknown size — that is heterogeneity.","idea":"Computer science asks what can be computed and how efficiently; software engineering asks how to deliver it to a cost, schedule and quality target with a team that will not stay forever. At scale, heterogeneity, legacy systems and constant business change defeat amateur programming, so process, documentation and explicit trade-offs appear — the written decisions that keep a large system buildable and changeable by people who never met its author. Because most lifetime cost is post-delivery, the discipline designs for change.","steps":[{"t":"Draw before you build","d":"Fix what the software must do, in writing, before arguing about code."},{"t":"Record every decision","d":"Requirements, designs and test records outlive the conversation that produced them."},{"t":"Leave access behind the wall","d":"Structure the code and the documents so a stranger can change one part without opening all of it."},{"t":"Trade qualities openly","d":"Maintainability, dependability, efficiency and acceptability cannot all be maximal; say which one you are buying."},{"t":"Inspect instead of hoping","d":"Reviews and tests replace 'it worked on my machine' with evidence someone else can check."}],"oneLiner":"Software engineering is the paperwork that keeps a big system changeable after the people who built it have left.","exam":"Start with the definition and the four fundamental activities: specification, development, validation, evolution. Add one sentence on why process beats heroics. The remaining marks come from naming the real challenges — heterogeneity, legacy systems, security, scale — and the fact that maintenance is about 60 per cent of lifetime cost."},{"n":2,"name":"Software Processes","hook":"A wedding kitchen that cooks the entire menu before tasting anything has bet the whole feast on one guess.","why":"Every project invents its own order of work, so nobody can plan, staff or repeat it, and lessons die with the project. Worse, when the order is wrong — testing only after everything is built — the expensive defects surface last, when a fix costs a hundred times more than it would have at the start.","story":"A wedding feast for five hundred is cooked in one of three ways. The first kitchen decides the whole menu in advance, cooks every dish in sequence and serves only when the last pot is done: one late change to the menu wastes the dal already boiled, and nobody tastes anything until the guests are seated. The second serves course by course, tasting and adjusting as it goes, so a bad batch is caught before it reaches the table. The third buys the achar and the momo from a specialist vendor and only assembles the plates — fast and cheap, but the vendor's recipe is not yours to change. Change the menu at four in the afternoon and see which kitchen survives.","idea":"A process model fixes the order of the same four activities — specification, design and implementation, validation, evolution — plus who does them and what evidence closes each stage. Which model you follow is decided by the stability of the requirements and the shape of the contract, not fashion: waterfall signs off each phase and delivers once, so it gives milestones and a fixed price but pushes all integration risk into late testing; incremental delivery ships prioritised, usable slices, absorbing change early at the cost of shared architecture, refactoring and repeated integration. Choosing against requirement volatility moves every correction to its most expensive point.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the sequence","d":"Name the activities and the artefact that ends each one, so work can be planned and repeated."},{"t":"Bet on change","d":"Stable requirements and fixed contracts suit waterfall; volatile ones suit incremental delivery."},{"t":"Serve in slices","d":"Each increment must be usable, so feedback arrives while changing it is still cheap."},{"t":"Buy the common parts","d":"Reuse and configure existing components when they are not your differentiator."},{"t":"Improve the kitchen","d":"Maturity models and retrospectives change the process itself, not just the current dish."}],"oneLiner":"A process model is a bet about how wrong your requirements will be; pick it by how much change you expect.","exam":"Draw waterfall with its five stages and give two advantages and two disadvantages, then incremental development. Extra marks come from saying when each fits — fixed-price contract versus volatile requirements — and from naming how to cope with change: prototyping, incremental delivery, refactoring, configuration management."},{"n":3,"name":"Agile Software Development","hook":"A shop that fills its storeroom once a year is guessing; the one that restocks every week is reading its customers.","why":"Requirements written once go stale while the software is being built: the market moves, the regulation changes, and the customer only understands what they wanted after seeing something that works. Heavy change control then makes every correction expensive, so people stop asking for corrections and the delivered system is correct and useless.","story":"Think of a kirana shop that fills its storeroom in Baishakh for the whole year. It has a careful list, a big discount on bulk rice, and no idea that by Kartik the neighbourhood wants instant noodles. The shop across the road buys every Sunday from what actually sold, so it never carries a year of the wrong thing. It keeps a running list of what customers asked for and could not get, ranked by what people ask for most. Each Sunday it fills the shelf with the top of that list, shows the customers, and hears what is still missing. Then the owner sits with the ledger and asks why the milk spoiled, before writing next week's list.","idea":"Agile keeps the work in short fixed windows that each end in something usable, and keeps the backlog ordered and open to change between windows. It fixes time and lets scope emerge, while a fixed-price contract fixes scope and price, so the two mechanisms contradict each other. Sprints re-rank the backlog and measure progress by working software, which leaves nothing stable to sign off and no up-front safety case; regulation needs requirements fixed, traceable and verified, so such projects keep plan-driven elements, and agile fails to scale without architecture and coordination.","steps":[{"t":"Rank the list by value","d":"The product backlog is one ordered list; business value, not whoever shouts loudest, sets the order."},{"t":"Freeze a short window","d":"A two to four week sprint: scope fixed inside, time fixed, so the team can finish something real. A fixed-price contract fixes scope and price at signature instead, so the two mechanisms contradict each other and nothing stable is left to sign off."},{"t":"Show a usable increment","d":"Every sprint ends in something demonstrable that meets an agreed definition of done."},{"t":"Let the customer re-rank","d":"New wishes go into the backlog for the next window, not into the one in progress. Progress is measured by working software rather than a signed specification, which leaves regulation nothing fixed, traceable and verified, so a safety case needs plan-driven elements alongside."},{"t":"Measure the pace","d":"Velocity and burndown show what is really getting done; the retrospective then fixes the process."}],"oneLiner":"Agile replaces one forecast made in advance with a forecast rewritten every two weeks from evidence.","exam":"Give the four manifesto values, then a plan-driven versus agile table. For full marks name Scrum roles, artefacts and ceremonies, or the XP practices, with one line on what each prevents. Close by saying when plan-driven still wins: fixed contracts, safety-critical systems, large distributed teams."},{"n":4,"name":"Requirements Engineering","hook":"The customer says 'a loan for my shop'; the bank's file must say how much, at what rate, repaid by when.","why":"The most expensive defect is a system built exactly to a specification that describes the wrong thing, because every later stage faithfully amplifies the error. People state wishes, not requirements: they contradict each other, leave out the obvious, and use words like fast and secure that nobody can test.","story":"Go to a bank for a shop loan and watch the file being built. You say you need money to stock the shop; the officer does not write that down, he asks what you sell, what your rent is, who will guarantee you, and what the central bank requires for unsecured lending. Your sentence becomes a numbered checklist with amounts and dates, because the branch manager upstairs will not read a story. A second officer reads the file for contradictions before it goes up, which is validation. When you later add your brother as a co-signer, the file is not rewritten from memory: every page is traced to the clause it affects and the rate is recalculated.","idea":"Requirements engineering turns wishes into numbered, testable statements by asking the right people, negotiating conflicts, writing the result precisely and tracking every later change. Functional requirements say what the system does, and a missing one shows up as a feature that simply fails; non-functional requirements say how well it does it, so they decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later. They are also the ones written vaguely, which makes them untestable, so each must be given a measurable metric.","steps":[{"t":"Ask, then watch","d":"Interviews and questionnaires collect opinions; observation and prototypes reveal what people actually do."},{"t":"Split wish from spec","d":"User requirements explain why; system requirements state measurable behaviour for developers. The non-functional ones decide the architecture and cannot be retrofitted by better coding later, so a missed one is the most expensive kind."},{"t":"Make it testable","d":"'The search is fast' becomes 'results return within two seconds for ten thousand records'."},{"t":"Validate against need","d":"Reviews, prototypes and generated test cases check validity, consistency and completeness."},{"t":"Trace every change","d":"One change request, an impact analysis over traced links, then a decision by the change board."}],"oneLiner":"A requirement is finished only when someone can write a test that would prove it was not met.","exam":"Define functional, non-functional and domain requirements with one example each, then list the process: elicitation, analysis and negotiation, specification, validation, management. Extra marks come from turning a vague non-functional wish into a measurable metric, naming elicitation techniques, and mentioning traceability and the change control board."},{"n":5,"name":"System Modeling","hook":"No single drawing explains a hospital: the referral letter, the OPD queue and the lab form each answer a different question.","why":"A system is too large for one head and too varied for one reader: the client cares about outcomes, the coder about classes, the database administrator about tables, the tester about states. Without a shared notation each builds a private picture, and the disagreements surface only at integration, which is exactly where they are most expensive to fix.","story":"Follow one patient through a Kathmandu hospital and count the pictures. The referral letter from the clinic says what this hospital is responsible for and what stays outside — that is the boundary; the token counter shows who the patient deals with and for what outcome. The OPD slip records the order of events: token, vitals, doctor, lab, pharmacy. The record room stores entities and their links, one visit carrying many prescriptions, each prescription many medicines. And the ward board tracks state: waiting, in consultation, admitted, discharged, with rules about which move is allowed from where. No one of these is the hospital; each drops what it does not need.","idea":"A model is a deliberate simplification: keep what answers one question, drop the rest, and draw it in a notation everyone reads the same way. Context fixes the boundary, interaction fixes who talks to whom and in what order, structural models fix what exists, behavioural models fix how it changes over time. Because every view hides what the next needs, the models must be kept consistent with each other, and that comparison is where contradictions surface.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the boundary","d":"A context diagram shows what is inside this system and which outside actors it must talk to."},{"t":"Show who wants what","d":"Use cases pair actors with goals; each case is one useful outcome for someone."},{"t":"Order the messages","d":"Sequence diagrams show time; class diagrams show structure, associations and multiplicity."},{"t":"Follow the states","d":"A state machine lets an event plus a guard select which transition is allowed."},{"t":"Use one notation","d":"UML shapes must mean the same thing to the client, the coder and the tester, and the models must be checked against each other, since every view hides what the next needs and that comparison is where contradictions surface."}],"oneLiner":"A model is not a smaller system; it is the part of the system that answers one question.","exam":"For the given system, draw a use case diagram with actors and goals, a class diagram with multiplicities, and a sequence diagram with message order. Extra marks come from correct notation — stick figures, solid versus dashed arrows, open arrowheads on associations — and from stating which model answers which question."},{"n":6,"name":"Architectural Design","hook":"You can repaint a hotel room in a week; you cannot move the lift shaft after the slab is poured.","why":"Performance, security and availability are properties of structure, not of careful coding: a chatty design cannot be optimised into a fast one. Get the shape wrong and every later feature fights it, so each change costs more, and the team ends up working around its own system instead of inside it.","story":"Plan a hotel and you decide where the lift, the kitchen, the plumbing shafts and the generator go before the first brick. After the slab is poured, a room can be repainted in a week but the lift cannot move at all. Floors stacked so each is served only by the one below give clean separation and slow service, since a request for a towel walks down every floor. A single central kitchen serving all rooms keeps the food consistent and makes the kitchen a bottleneck. A menu board, a kitchen and a waiter split the same job three ways, so the menu can change without rebuilding the kitchen. And the laundry can be an outside vendor under contract: quick to replace, impossible to supervise closely.","idea":"Architectural design fixes the components, their responsibilities and the connections they are allowed before anyone writes detail code. The non-functional requirement the system cannot afford to lose decides, because each pattern buys one quality at the price of another: layering buys substitutability and adds latency; a repository buys shared consistent data and creates a bottleneck; client-server centralises control and creates one point of failure. The choice is defensible only when the accepted cost is written down.","steps":[{"t":"Name the quality that wins","d":"Decide which non-functional property dominates before drawing anything."},{"t":"Draw boxes and allowed arrows","d":"Give each component one responsibility and forbid the shortcuts that bypass layers."},{"t":"Choose a known shape","d":"Layered, client-server, MVC, pipe and filter, repository, or services."},{"t":"Write the trade-off down","d":"Every pattern buys one quality and costs another; record which cost you accepted."},{"t":"Describe it in views","d":"Logical, process, development and physical views, held together by scenarios."}],"oneLiner":"Architecture is the set of decisions that are expensive to reverse, and patterns are the ones others already paid for.","exam":"Name any four patterns, draw each as boxes and arrows, and give one advantage, one disadvantage and one system where it fits. Extra marks come from tying every choice to a non-functional requirement, and from mentioning architectural design decisions and the 4+1 views."},{"n":7,"name":"Design and Implementation","hook":"A cycle mended with welded parts runs today and cannot be repaired next year.","why":"Code is read and changed far more than it is first written, so a design where every part reaches into every other turns a one-line change into a tour of the system. Without version control and a shared standard, two people fix the same file and one fix silently disappears, and the code drifts until nobody dares touch it.","story":"Watch a good cycle mechanic in Asan and you see design decisions, not just repairs. He builds the wheel so a brake block can be unbolted and swapped in minutes, and he refuses the cheap frame where the cable is welded along the tube, because one worn cable would scrap the whole machine. He groups the gears together on one hub and keeps them away from the bell, so a change to gearing never touches the bell. For the derailleur he does not file teeth by hand: every frame carries the standard hanger, so any brand's unit bolts straight on. And he keeps a register of which cycle has which part, so the replacement he fitted last week can be found again.","idea":"Design sets the seams: what a part hides inside and what it promises outside. Cohesion measures whether the responsibilities inside a component belong together, so high cohesion gives one reason to change; coupling measures how much one component depends on another, so low coupling stops a change from rippling outward. Information hiding reinforces both by exposing only a stable interface, and the consequence is that a part can be replaced without opening the system. Implementation keeps those seams honest with version control, review and refactoring.","steps":[{"t":"Find the parts","d":"Nouns in the requirements become candidate classes; group what changes together."},{"t":"Hide the inside","d":"Expose an interface and keep fields private, so callers cannot depend on details."},{"t":"Aim high cohesion, low coupling","d":"One part, one reason to change; few and narrow links between parts."},{"t":"Reuse a known seam","d":"Apply a pattern or a library instead of inventing a new shape."},{"t":"Version, review, refactor","d":"Baselines, code review and continuous refactoring stop the seams from rotting."}],"oneLiner":"Good design is knowing where the cuts go: parts you can lift out without rebuilding the machine.","exam":"Define cohesion, coupling and encapsulation with a concrete example, then name four design patterns with one line of intent and one real use each. Extra marks come from explaining why each pattern lowers coupling, and from listing configuration management: version control, baseline, build and release."},{"n":8,"name":"Software Testing","hook":"A bus can pass every workshop check and still be too tall for the bridge on its route.","why":"Programmers test the paths they had in mind, so their own assumptions survive unnoticed, and a demo that works on one desk proves almost nothing. A defect found in production costs far more than the same defect found at the desk, and each fix can break something else, so checks must be planned, repeatable and re-run.","story":"Take a new bus built at a workshop in Pokhara. Each brake cylinder is tested alone on the bench, with a dummy pedal standing in for the rest of the system. Then the parts are bolted together and the joints are checked, because that is where leaks appear. The assembled bus is driven fully loaded up the Prithvi highway, which tests the whole vehicle against what the route demands, not against what the drawing says. Finally Sajha's inspector and a real driver sign it off — and the driver may still refuse it. When the wiper is fixed, the brakes are tested again, because the same wiring loom was disturbed.","idea":"Testing compares what the system does against what it was supposed to do, with the expectation taken from the specification rather than from the code's own logic. Coverage counts the structure the tests walked through, not the requirements the system had to meet: a suite written from existing code cannot cover an omitted requirement, a wrong assumption about the user, or a timing and load fault that appears only when the whole system runs. Coverage is therefore a measure of confidence, not proof, and inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary.","steps":[{"t":"Test the part alone","d":"Unit tests with stubs and drivers, plus white-box coverage of statements and branches. Coverage counts the paths the code contains, not the requirements it was meant to meet, so a branch-complete suite can still miss an omitted requirement, a wrong user assumption, or a load fault. It is a measure of confidence, not proof, so inspection plus system and acceptance testing remain necessary."},{"t":"Test the joints","d":"Integration: top-down with stubs, bottom-up with drivers, or big bang."},{"t":"Test against requirements","d":"System and release testing: black-box cases plus performance, security and recovery."},{"t":"Let the user decide","d":"Acceptance testing: alpha in house, beta with real users on their own data."},{"t":"Write the test first","d":"TDD red, green, refactor, then regression after every later change."}],"oneLiner":"Testing cannot prove a system has no bugs; it can only show which risks have actually been looked at.","exam":"Open with a verification versus validation table, then the levels in order and one thing each catches. Name black-box and white-box techniques with an example case. Extra marks come from the TDD cycle with one benefit and one limitation, and from explaining why regression follows every fix."},{"n":9,"name":"Software Evolution","hook":"The jeep that has carried goods to the village for twenty years is held together by patches, and cannot be retired.","why":"Software that is used will be changed, because the business, the platform and the law all keep moving. If each change is an emergency patch with nothing written down, the structure erodes, the original designers leave, and the system becomes too valuable to scrap and too expensive to touch — a legacy that quietly holds the organisation hostage.","story":"Consider the Land Rover that has run goods to a hill village since 1995. It was specified for a lighter load and a road that no longer exists; now it carries cement, and the springs are packed with extra leaves to cope. Every breakdown is mended in the bazaar with whatever fits, so the wiring has grown a second loom nobody has drawn. The driver refuses a new dashboard because he knows where every switch is, so improvements are resisted even when they are good. Nobody scraps it, because the village's whole supply line assumes it. The choices are to keep patching, rebuild it onto a new chassis, bolt a modern body over the old one, or buy a new jeep and retrain the driver.","idea":"Evolution treats change as the normal case: a request is logged, analysed for what else it touches, planned into a release, implemented and tested. Maintenance is classified by purpose — corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive — because each needs a different share of the budget. Most post-delivery change is not repair but improvement: once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reporting, so perfective maintenance is about half of maintenance effort, against roughly twenty per cent corrective and twenty-five adaptive. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, structure and documented design decide whether that demand can be met affordably.","steps":[{"t":"Log the request, not the patch","d":"A change request plus impact analysis shows what else the change will disturb."},{"t":"Classify by purpose","d":"Corrective, adaptive, perfective, preventive: different work, different budget. Perfective takes about half because once a system works, users ask for more functions, better performance and new reports, so most post-delivery change is improvement rather than repair."},{"t":"Refactor to fight complexity","d":"Structure is rebuilt continuously, or each change makes the next one harder. Since maintenance is about sixty per cent of lifetime cost, documented design decides whether that demand can be met affordably."},{"t":"Judge legacy by value and quality","d":"Keep, re-engineer, wrap behind an interface, migrate, or scrap."},{"t":"Release in planned steps","d":"Test, version and retire gradually instead of switching everything at once."}],"oneLiner":"A system nobody planned to change is a system that will soon be too expensive to change.","exam":"List the four maintenance types with rough percentages and one example each, then the legacy strategies: scrap, keep, re-engineer, wrap, migrate. Extra marks come from naming Lehman's laws, especially continuing change and increasing complexity, and from placing impact analysis inside the evolution process."},{"n":10,"name":"Software Management","hook":"Doubling the guests does not double the cooks, and doubling the cooks does not halve the time.","why":"Software is invisible, so a project can look finished for months and then miss by a year. Cost is almost entirely people, so an estimate that is half wrong is a business failure, not a technical one, and panic hiring near the deadline makes it later still because new people need the old people's time to learn.","story":"Book a caterer for a wedding of four hundred and watch the quote being built. She does not guess one number: she lists every dish, every utensil and every hand, then prices the job from the count. She knows six hundred guests need more than one and a half times the work of four hundred: bigger pots, longer queues, no spare table. She writes down what could ruin the day — the vegetable supplier striking, the gas running out, rain on the cooking yard — and decides in advance which risks she absorbs and which the family must. On the day she checks the count against the plan every hour. And she keeps one master recipe, so three cooks cannot produce three different dals.","idea":"Management turns a wish into commitments: break the work down until each piece can be estimated, estimate by two independent methods, schedule the dependencies, keep a ranked risk list, and compare actual progress against the plan often enough to act. Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not with the code; COCOMO expresses this through an exponent above one, so doubling size more than doubles effort, and duration rises only as the effort raised to a fraction, so a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone.","steps":[{"t":"Break the work down","d":"A work breakdown structure until every task is small enough to estimate and assign."},{"t":"Estimate twice","d":"Expert judgement or analogy, plus an algorithmic model such as COCOMO, then reconcile the two. Effort grows faster than size because communication, coordination and rework grow with the number of people, not the code. COCOMO's exponent above one (1.05 organic, 1.12 semi-detached, 1.20 embedded) makes doubling size more than double effort, and its duration exponent below one means a late large project cannot be rescued by schedule alone."},{"t":"Schedule the dependencies","d":"Gantt bars, milestones and the critical path show what actually delays delivery."},{"t":"Rank the risks","d":"Probability times impact, an owner, and a response: avoid, reduce, transfer, accept."},{"t":"Measure against the plan","d":"Actual versus planned progress, quality reviews and configuration control."}],"oneLiner":"Effort grows faster than size, so a plan is only honest while it is still being measured against reality.","exam":"Define project management, list the planning steps, then compute COCOMO effort as a times KLOC to the power b, showing the substitution before the arithmetic. Extra marks come from the risk process, from explaining why adding people to a late project delays it further, and from WBS or a Gantt chart."}],"cdc":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to Compiler","hook":"A letter written in Nepali must reach a reader who knows only English, and no single clerk can do the whole job.","why":"Hand-writing machine code for every program and every processor is impossible at scale, and a direct source-to-machine translator is worse: it hides every error until the last moment and must be rewritten for each language-and-machine pair. Ten languages and five processors need fifty translators, each with its own bugs and none sharing an optimiser.","story":"Think of a translation office turning a Nepali letter into an English legal document. The first clerk reads the running handwriting and separates it into words and punctuation, writing each on a slip with its category. The second checks those slips against a grammar rulebook and rebuilds the sentences, rejecting anything illegal. The third checks that names, titles and dates exist and agree. Then the head clerk writes the meaning in plain neutral English carrying the grammar of neither language. A style editor tightens that draft without changing its meaning, and a typist sets it on the final letterhead. No clerk understands more than one job, and the neutral draft is what lets any Nepali desk serve any English desk.","idea":"A compiler is a pipeline of small specialists, each taking one representation and producing a simpler or more machine-like one. No single translator can be built, tested and retargeted, but one phase's output is the documented input of the next, so each can be proved and marked alone. Splitting at a machine-independent form lets n languages and m machines cost n plus m pieces instead of n times m translators, and each error is reported by the phase that can name it.","steps":[{"t":"Break characters into tokens","d":"The lexer groups characters into names, numbers and operators, dropping whitespace and comments."},{"t":"Check the grammar","d":"The parser proves the token sequence is derivable from the grammar and builds a parse tree."},{"t":"Check the meaning","d":"Semantic analysis resolves declarations and types, annotating the tree and filling the symbol table."},{"t":"Write a neutral draft","d":"Intermediate code generation emits machine-independent instructions from the annotated tree."},{"t":"Tighten the draft","d":"The optimiser rewrites the intermediate code to do less work with the same result."},{"t":"Set the final type","d":"The code generator chooses instructions and registers for one particular machine."}],"oneLiner":"A compiler is a chain of single-job clerks joined by a neutral draft that belongs to no language and no machine.","exam":"Draw the six phases as one vertical diagram with the input and output of each box written beside it, then bracket the first three as the front end and the last three as the back end. Finish with the n times m versus n plus m argument for retargeting; that separates a list of phases from an answer."},{"n":2,"name":"Lexical Analysis and Syntax Analysis","hook":"At the airport one officer only stamps slips, and the officer behind her decides whether the whole trip is legal.","why":"Sentence rules and word rules are different kinds of rules, and a scanner that tries to do both becomes unmaintainable: every grammar change forces a rewrite of the character handling. But the split is forced by something deeper. Grouping characters needs only a fixed amount of memory, while brackets, ifs and calls nest to any depth, and no fixed-state machine can count unbounded nesting.","story":"The arrivals hall at Tribhuvan airport. The first officer reads a continuous stream of passengers and issues one slip per person: NEPALI, passport 1234; TOURIST, visa 90 days. He never decides whether a trip is legal, he only classifies, and because his manual is a fixed list of patterns he never remembers more than which of a dozen stamps he is halfway through. That bounded memory is why a finite automaton suffices. Behind him a supervisor takes the sequence of slips and checks it against the rulebook: every entry must have an exit, a visa must be followed by a stamp. She keeps a stack of rules waiting to be closed, because rules nest, and that stack is the one thing the first officer lacks.","idea":"Token shapes are regular, so a finite automaton recognises them with bounded memory. Sentence structure is context-free, so the parser needs a stack to match nested constructs. Splitting the two lets the scanner be generated from regular expressions and the parser from a grammar, and keeps each error message at the right level.","steps":[{"t":"Describe tokens as regexes","d":"Each token class gets a pattern, and the longest match wins over the shortest."},{"t":"Build the recogniser","d":"Thompson's construction makes an NFA, subset construction merges it into a DFA, minimisation shrinks the table."},{"t":"Scan into tokens","d":"A buffer with two pointers walks the input once and emits name-and-attribute pairs."},{"t":"Match tokens to the grammar","d":"The parser shifts tokens onto a stack and reduces them by productions, or predicts the next production."},{"t":"Build the parse tree","d":"Each reduction or prediction records how the sentence was derived."},{"t":"Recover at the first error","d":"Panic mode skips to a synchronising token so checking can continue."}],"oneLiner":"The scanner classifies with bounded memory; the parser proves the sequence legal with a stack, because grammar nests.","exam":"For the numeric half, convert the given regular expression to an NFA, then a DFA, then minimise it, showing the state sets at each step. For the theory half, say why a finite automaton cannot match nested parentheses and what the parser's stack adds. Naming a token as a pair of name and attribute value earns the first easy mark."},{"n":3,"name":"Syntax-Directed Translation","hook":"The drawing proves the house is legal, but nothing in it yet says how much brick to order.","why":"A parse tree only answers yes or no. Every later phase needs numbers attached to it: the type of a subexpression, the width of an array, the label a jump should target. Walking the tree again with hand-written code duplicates the grammar in two places that drift apart, and computing values in an arbitrary order can use an attribute before it has been given a value.","story":"A crew building a house from a drawing. The drawing decides only that the house is legal: walls sit on floors, doors sit in walls. The numbers that make it real are pencilled on the joints, and they travel in two directions. A wall's area is measured from the bricks below it, so that number rises from the parts to the whole. The ceiling height, by contrast, is decided at the top and handed down, and a door frame must be shorter than the wall on its left, so that number travels sideways or downward. Write the rule for each joint on the drawing and a surveyor can price the whole house in one walk: bottom-up if every number only rises, left to right if numbers also come down.","idea":"A syntax-directed definition attaches a semantic rule to every production. Attributes are synthesized when their value comes from children and inherited when it comes from the parent or an earlier sibling. If every attribute is synthesized, one postorder walk suffices; if inherited values depend only on the left, one left-to-right pass suffices, and that restricted form is what top-down translators can run.","steps":[{"t":"Give symbols attributes","d":"Decide what each grammar symbol must carry, such as type, place or label."},{"t":"Write rules per production","d":"Each production says how the parent's attributes are computed from the children's."},{"t":"Draw the dependency graph","d":"An edge joins two attributes when one is needed to compute the other."},{"t":"Order the evaluation","d":"A topological sort of that graph gives a safe order, if one exists."},{"t":"Restrict to one pass","d":"Only-synthesized gives S-attributed, left-dependent gives L-attributed, so no graph is needed."},{"t":"Read the root value","d":"The translation is the attribute left on the start symbol."}],"oneLiner":"An SDD writes the meaning on the parse tree; the direction the numbers travel decides the evaluation order.","exam":"Draw the annotated parse tree for a small expression and label every attribute as synthesized or inherited, then state the order of evaluation and justify it from the dependency graph. The second half is the one-pass restriction: name the L-attributed condition of depending only on the parent and left siblings."},{"n":4,"name":"Type Checking","hook":"A shop ledger that happily adds metres of pipe to kilograms of nails produces totals that look fine and a site that fails.","why":"Machine instructions are typed. Adding the bit pattern of a float as if it were an integer, or dereferencing an integer as a pointer, does not raise an error, it silently produces garbage that surfaces far from its cause. Finding such a mismatch at run time means the customer finds it, and the report points at the symptom rather than the line that was wrong.","story":"A hardware shop keeps a ledger for a building site. The clerk may add three metres of pipe to two metres of pipe. He may not add metres to kilograms, and if a contractor insists, the clerk writes the conversion down and both sign, because something is lost. If the shop allowed any quantity to be added to any other, the totals would add up and the mistake would appear only when the plumbing is installed. So each line carries a unit tag, the tag is checked before the addition, and the clerk converts only in the safe direction, metres to centimetres and never silently back. Records are the subtle case: the pipe's diameter is not a pipe, so the ledger must open the entry and read its fields first.","idea":"Types are the units of a program. A grammar constrains shape, not meaning, so adding a string to an integer derives from the addition and assignment productions while the parser accepts it. A type system gives every expression a type expression and demands that the operands of an operator agree, inserting only safe widening conversions and rejecting the rest unless the programmer writes an explicit cast. The mismatch is caught before code exists, so the error names its line instead of appearing as garbage at run time.","steps":[{"t":"Record declared types","d":"Each declaration puts a type expression into the symbol table."},{"t":"Build type expressions","d":"Arrays, pointers, records and functions are composed from basic types with constructors."},{"t":"Infer each expression","d":"Rules attached to productions give the type of a node from the types of its children."},{"t":"Require compatibility","d":"Operands must be the same type, or one must widen safely into the other. The grammar only constrains shape, not meaning: count + 'abc' derives from the addition and assignment productions exactly as 2 + 3 does, so the parser accepts it and only these type rules reject it."},{"t":"Insert coercions","d":"The compiler adds widening conversions silently and demands a cast for narrowing."},{"t":"Report or reject","d":"A mismatch stops with a message naming both types and the expression."}],"oneLiner":"Types are units: check the tag before every operation, convert only upward for free, and make the lossy direction explicit.","exam":"Write the rule for addition as a table row: two operand types in, one result type out, with the mismatch case named. Then give one line each on widening versus narrowing and on structural versus name equivalence. The rule row carries most of the marks; the two distinctions are the short tail of the answer."},{"n":5,"name":"Symbol Table Design","hook":"The guest house book is asked the same question by reception, kitchen and laundry, and must answer all three the same way.","why":"Names are mentioned long before and after declaration, and every phase asks: is this declared, what is its type, how many bytes, which scope. Without one shared store each phase rescans the source and the answers drift apart. Searching the whole name list on every question makes compilation of a large file quadratic, and naive deletion at a block's end destroys an outer name still in use.","story":"The register of a Kathmandu guest house. Every guest is entered in one large book: name, room, identity number, checkout date. When a new floor opens the desk starts a fresh page instead of rewriting the book, and if a second Bikash checks in, the newest page wins until he leaves, when that page is closed and the older Bikash is visible again under the same name. The book would be useless if the clerk read from the first page on every question, so the desk keeps an index at the back that jumps straight to the right line by name. Reception, kitchen and laundry all consult that one book, which is why the room number on a dinner tray always matches the room at the desk.","idea":"A symbol table is one shared map from identifier to attributes, with insert, lookup and scope-exit delete. A hash table gives near-constant access in practice; nesting is handled by a stack of tables, or one table with a scope field and an undo log, so an inner declaration shadows an outer one and disappears cleanly when its block ends.","steps":[{"t":"Open a table per scope","d":"Each block or procedure pushes its own namespace onto a stack."},{"t":"Insert with attributes","d":"Name, kind, type, size, offset and scope are stored together at declaration."},{"t":"Index by hash","d":"A hash function turns the name into a bucket so lookup skips most entries."},{"t":"Resolve collisions","d":"Chaining or open addressing keeps distinct names that hash alike."},{"t":"Look up innermost first","d":"The newest scope is searched before outer ones, which gives shadowing for free."},{"t":"Undo on scope exit","d":"Entries of the closed scope are removed, revealing the outer declaration again."}],"oneLiner":"One shared register of names, indexed by hash and stacked by scope, so every phase gets the same answer cheaply.","exam":"Draw one entry with its fields, then a hash table with chaining and a worked collision, then say in one sentence what happens at scope exit. Add the comparison line: average constant lookup against linear search. The diagram plus the shadowing sentence is the shape of a full answer."},{"n":6,"name":"Run-time Storage Management","hook":"The auditorium rents blocks of seats, and the last troupe to arrive is always the first to leave.","why":"A recursive procedure needs a separate copy of its locals on every call, so one fixed area cannot hold them. Worse, a frame must stay alive while it calls another, and the callee may need to reach variables of the procedure that lexically encloses it, not the one that called it. Without a disciplined layout and the right links, recursion overwrites its own data and nested scopes cannot be resolved.","story":"An auditorium that rents blocks of seats. A troupe arrives, gets a block for itself and its props, performs, and leaves, and because troupes leave in reverse order of arrival the manager needs one marker for where free seats begin. That marker is a stack. Each block lists what it brought: return address, saved registers, parameters, locals, temporaries. A troupe invited by another keeps a pointer to the inviter's block, which is how it returns a result; a troupe detached from a parent office keeps a second pointer to that office, which is how a dancer reaches a prop owned by the office, not by whoever called. Props that outlive the show go to the standing area at the back, handed out and returned in any order, where gaps appear.","idea":"Every active call owns an activation record pushed on a stack, because calls nest and return in reverse order. The record holds the return address, saved registers, parameters, locals, temporaries and two links: the control link to the caller and the access link to the lexically enclosing scope. Objects that outlive the nesting go on the heap, which fragments and must be freed or collected.","steps":[{"t":"Push a frame on call","d":"The stack pointer moves by the known frame size before the body runs."},{"t":"Fill parameters and return address","d":"The caller places arguments and the resume point where the callee expects them."},{"t":"Set both links","d":"The control link points at the caller, the access link at the enclosing scope."},{"t":"Allocate locals and temporaries","d":"Offsets inside the frame are fixed at compile time, so no search is needed."},{"t":"Reach non-locals by link","d":"A chain of access links leads to the frame that owns the variable."},{"t":"Pop on return","d":"The frame is discarded at once, which is why recursion is cheap here."}],"oneLiner":"Each call gets a frame on a stack because calls nest, with one link to the caller and one to the enclosing scope.","exam":"Draw a single activation record with every field labelled, then trace two recursive calls and mark where the second frame sits relative to the first. The mark most answers lose is the difference between the control link and the access link; add one closing line on heap garbage and dangling pointers."},{"n":7,"name":"Intermediate Code Generation","hook":"The order says steam the momos; no single cook can execute a sentence like that, so it becomes numbered steps.","why":"An expression nests to any depth, so no one instruction can represent it, and mapping source straight onto machine code welds the language to the processor: every optimisation would have to be rewritten for each target. Without named temporaries there is no way to say that two subexpressions produced the same value, so nothing can be shared, reordered or deleted.","story":"A busy momo kitchen. The order arrives as one line, steam the spicy chicken momos, which nobody can execute directly. The head cook rewrites it as numbered steps, each doing exactly one thing: chop onion into bowl t1, mix t1 with the mince into bowl t2, fold the filling into t3, steam t3. Because every bowl has a name, a second batch that needs the same filling reuses bowl t2 instead of chopping again. The ticket rail records four columns per step: action, first ingredient, second ingredient, bowl. A shorthand version numbers the steps instead of the bowls, which saves labels but makes moving a step awkward, so the kitchen also keeps a separate slip listing the order in which the tickets are to be read.","idea":"Lower every expression into instructions with at most one operator, each writing a named temporary. Nothing in that form mentions a processor, so one optimiser and one set of analyses serve every target, and identical subtrees can share a single computed value. Quadruples name the result explicitly, triples use the instruction number, and indirect triples add a pointer list so that steps can be reordered.","steps":[{"t":"Walk the annotated tree","d":"Each node knows the place where its value will live."},{"t":"Emit one operation per node","d":"Operands are read from places and the result is written to a new temporary."},{"t":"Name each result","d":"Temporaries turn a nested expression into a flat list of simple steps."},{"t":"Encode as quadruples","d":"Operator, two arguments and a result make each step a row."},{"t":"Share identical subtrees","d":"Reusing one temporary for a repeated computation is the first optimisation."},{"t":"Leave jumps for later","d":"Labels are emitted, but targets of forward jumps are patched afterwards."}],"oneLiner":"Flatten the expression into one-operator steps with named bowls, so any back end can cook the same recipe.","exam":"Generate the three-address code for the given expression, then re-encode exactly those lines as quadruples and as triples in a table with headers. The marks are in the headers and the numbering. Add why triples are hard to reorder and how indirect triples repair that for the final two marks."},{"n":8,"name":"Code Generation","hook":"The recipe says mix, but the kitchen must still decide which pan is already hot on the stove.","why":"Intermediate code says what to compute, never how. The target has a few registers, many addressing modes and instructions of very different cost, so a naive mapping loads and stores every operand and produces code several times slower and larger. Registers are the scarcest resource, and deciding badly what stays in one is the difference between tight code and a program that spends its life in memory.","story":"The same recipe in two kitchens. The instructions say mix t1 with t2; they do not say whether to use the big pan already hot on the burner or fetch a clean one from the shelf. A cook who returns every ingredient to the shelf after each step and fetches it again is correct and hopelessly slow, which is a compiler that stores every temporary to memory. A cook who keeps the working mixture on the heat and shelves something only when every burner is full is the register allocator, and the act of shelving is a spill. Within one uninterrupted stretch with no decision interrupting, the cook can notice that the same chopped onions feed two dishes and chop once, and can drop a dish nobody ordered.","idea":"Code generation chooses, for each intermediate instruction, real instructions and a home for every value. Basic blocks and their DAG show what is reused and what is dead inside a straight run; register allocation is an assignment of a small set of registers to many live values, solved by graph colouring or by heuristics with next-use information, spilling to memory when the pressure is too high. The first instruction, every jump target and every instruction immediately after a jump is a leader, so a jump may enter only at a leader and no branch lands in the middle of the block.","steps":[{"t":"Find the basic blocks","d":"The first instruction, every jump target and every instruction after a jump starts a block."},{"t":"Link them into a flow graph","d":"Edges show which block can follow which, so loops and branches become visible."},{"t":"Build the block DAG","d":"Repeated subexpressions share a node and unused nodes drop out."},{"t":"Select instructions","d":"Each node maps to one or more target instructions, cheapest first."},{"t":"Allocate registers","d":"Values that are live together must not share a register, or one is spilled."},{"t":"Emit the code","d":"Address descriptors and register descriptors say where each value currently lives."}],"oneLiner":"Code generation is choosing real utensils: which values stay hot in registers, which get shelved, and which work is repeated.","exam":"For the numeric part, split the given code into basic blocks by the leader rules, draw the flow graph and then the DAG of one block. For the theory, define getReg and give one spill decision. The leaders are the first mark; the shared node in the DAG is what shows the block was really understood."},{"n":9,"name":"Code Optimization","hook":"A porter carries the same sack of rice up the hill twice because nobody told him it was already at the lodge.","why":"People write for other people: a subexpression repeats because the formula reads better, a copy gives a value a readable name, a constant is loaded at run time, and an unchanging value is recomputed on every loop pass. The cost is real and repeated. Yet careless rewriting is worse than none: moving a load past a store or deleting code with a side effect quietly changes what the program does. And a rewrite must be profitable: the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space, so the saving has to repay that cost, and it must never move an error message or hide a fault the unoptimised code would have raised.","story":"A porter supplying a trekking lodge. On the first climb he carries a sack of rice up, comes down, and carries it up again because nobody told him it was already in the store: a common subexpression. He also carries a crate the kitchen never opens: dead code. He weighs each sack at the top of the pass though its weight was known at the shop: constant folding. Halfway up he sees the last stretch is a staircase and takes a smaller basket, which is strength reduction. Every trip he fetches the same heavy lantern though it is needed only at the lodge, so he moves it out of the climb. At the door, one glance at his last two steps removes a pointless step back and forth: a peephole.","idea":"Optimisation removes work the source does not require, but only when the visible result stays identical: it must preserve observable behaviour, so the result and every visible side effect stay the same. It must be profitable, since the analysis and rewriting consume compile time and code space and must repay that cost, and it must not change when errors are reported or hide a fault the source would have raised. Local rewrites need little proof, while global rewrites need data-flow facts to show that a value is still the same one; only then is a rewrite such as constant folding or hoisting a loop-invariant expression applied.","steps":[{"t":"Build the flow graph","d":"Loops and branches are located so that local and global rewrites can be told apart."},{"t":"Compute the facts","d":"Reaching definitions, available expressions and liveness say what is true at each point."},{"t":"Rewrite inside blocks","d":"The DAG shares repeated subexpressions and removes values nobody uses."},{"t":"Rewrite across blocks","d":"Invariant work leaves loops, copies propagate, dead definitions disappear."},{"t":"Run the peephole","d":"Adjacent target instructions are rewritten into fewer or cheaper ones."},{"t":"Repeat until stable","d":"One pass exposes opportunities for the next, so iterate to a fixed point."}],"oneLiner":"Optimisation is not doing unnecessary work, and it is legal only when the observable result is bit for bit the same.","exam":"For the applied question, draw the DAG of the given block, write the optimised code beneath the original and name each transformation used. Naming the transformation earns as much as performing it. Close with one sentence per rewrite on why it preserves meaning, which is what the examiner is checking."},{"n":10,"name":"Case Studies: C and C++ Compilers","hook":"Two intake desks, one for plain letters and one for legal documents full of cross-references, feed a single workshop.","why":"C++ decides almost everything at compile time and leaves none of it in the object file: template blanks must be filled before the code even exists, two overloads need distinguishable names, and a virtual call needs a table built at run time. If each language carried its own optimiser and back end, the same work would be duplicated and every new processor would need a new compiler for every language.","story":"A translation agency with two desks and one back room. The C desk takes short plain letters: read, check, hand them through. The C++ desk takes legal documents full of boilerplate and cross-references. A template is a blank form that must be filled in before the document can be read at all, and two clauses with identical wording but different annexes cannot share a filing name, so each gets a coded reference that survives into the archive. Clauses readable at two levels get a bookmark table, so the reader jumps to the right version only when the document is used. Both desks hand the back room the same internal draft, so one team improves the workshop, and a new press needs only a new operator, never a new agency.","idea":"Split the compiler into a language-specific front end, a shared optimiser over a language-independent form, and a machine-specific back end. C and C++ differ almost entirely in the front end, where templates are instantiated, overloads resolved and names mangled; the SSA-based middle end and the register-allocating back end are the same machinery.","steps":[{"t":"Parse and lower","d":"Both front ends produce a language-independent tree, GENERIC in GCC's case."},{"t":"Resolve the hard name work","d":"Templates instantiate, overloads resolve, and names are mangled so both languages can link."},{"t":"Simplify to GIMPLE","d":"The tree becomes a flat three-address form, then static single assignment."},{"t":"Run shared optimisations","d":"Constant propagation, dead code removal, inlining and loop passes are language-blind."},{"t":"Lower to the machine form","d":"The optimised IR expands into RTL, close to real instructions and registers."},{"t":"Allocate and emit","d":"Registers are assigned, instructions scheduled, and assembly written out."}],"oneLiner":"C and C++ differ in the front end and share the optimiser and back end, which is why one team's work serves both.","exam":"Draw the pipeline as three bands and put all C++-specific work in the first band only, or the diagram contradicts itself. Name two concrete front-end jobs, template instantiation and name mangling, and explain in one sentence why the middle end can be shared across languages and machines."}],"egov":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction to E-Government and E-Governance","hook":"A ward office paints its counter, adds a computer, and the same clerk still decides who waits.","why":"If a country only computerises the counter, the queue moves but the decision stays hidden. Forms go online while the clerk keeps the discretion, so speed improves and trust does not. The money is spent, the citizen is still a supplicant, and nobody can prove how a file was decided.","story":"Walk into a ward office in Kathmandu. At the counter you hand over papers and receive a recommendation letter, and that is the service. Behind the counter sits a register where the clerk writes what he decided and why, a noticeboard where the ward budget is pinned, and a complaint box nobody has opened in months. Paint the counter and add a printer, and the service is faster; the register, the noticeboard and the box are untouched, so power has not moved an inch. Open the register to the public and the same clerk begins writing differently, because now the reason is visible.","idea":"E-government is ICT at the delivery edge, the output leg where a certificate leaves the building. E-governance is ICT across the whole loop: the inputs, the decision rule, the output, and the complaint that feeds back. That is why one is a subset of the other, and why a portal alone cannot fix a dishonest register.","steps":[{"t":"Computerise the counter","d":"Start where the citizen meets the state, and notice what the counter still cannot do on its own."},{"t":"Trace the loop behind it","d":"Follow one request backwards: citizen data in, rules and files through, certificate out, complaint back. The loop, not the counter, is what a service really is."},{"t":"Open the loop outward","d":"Publish the decision rule and the budget, and let the complaint return as evidence. This is the step that changes power rather than speed."},{"t":"Repeat it as a life cycle","d":"Initiation, planning, implementation, operation, monitoring, improvement. Each service becomes the input to the next, so maturity is a loop and not a launch."},{"t":"Judge it by actor","d":"Citizens gain time, government gains data, society gains trust, and the digital divide quietly decides who gains nothing."}],"oneLiner":"E-government moves the counter online; e-governance puts the register, the budget and the complaint box there too.","exam":"For the standard compare question, lead with the scope test, service delivery versus the whole governing process, then one Nepali example on each side and the benefits per actor. The extra marks come from naming a barrier the distinction explains, such as a portal that hides a discretionary register."},{"n":2,"name":"Models of E-Governance","hook":"Five models, five levels, but only two questions: which way does the information go, and who moves first?","why":"Memorised as ten separate lists, the models blur by the third one and the levels collapse into better websites. Then an unseen case cannot be placed at all: is a district ranking a model or a level? Without a direction and an initiator, the answer is guesswork, and the marks go to whoever guessed the textbook order.","story":"Stand at the passport office wall. There is a noticeboard of fees and holidays, which speaks to everybody and hears nothing back. There is a photocopy of the tender file that only the auditor and one journalist ever ask for. There is a chart of passports issued per branch, printed so that the worst branch can see itself. There is a delegation of applicants waiting for the chief to complain about the queue. And there is the counter where you pay, submit, and later check your file. Five things on one wall, and each is a different direction of talk.","idea":"The five models are the directions information can travel between state and citizen. Broadcasting pushes one way to everyone and hears nothing back; comparative analysis reuses the same published data as a ranking, so the comparison itself supplies pressure; critical flow aims the push at auditors and press; mobilisation reverses the arrow; interactive service closes it with a transaction the back office records. The five levels then measure who initiates and how far the transaction runs: the state publishes, the citizen asks, the citizen completes, and finally the state acts before being asked.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the direction first","d":"Every model is one arrow. Ask who sends and who receives before naming it, and the five stop looking arbitrary."},{"t":"Push out, hear nothing","d":"Broadcasting is the noticeboard: cheap, wide, and blind, because no reply path exists."},{"t":"Aim where someone can punish","d":"Critical flow sends the sensitive file to auditors, courts and press, since disclosure only works where a reader has power to act."},{"t":"Turn data into a mirror","d":"Comparative analysis ranks branches, districts or years, and the comparison itself supplies the pressure to improve."},{"t":"Let citizens push back","d":"Mobilisation and lobbying run the same arrow the other way: petitions, consultations and campaigns that set the agenda."},{"t":"Close the loop, then climb","d":"Interactive service is the only two-way model. The ladder then asks who starts, the state, the citizen, or the system before either asks."}],"oneLiner":"Five models are five arrows; five levels are who moves first, the state, the citizen, or the system.","exam":"Draw the arrow before writing the name. For each model give direction, purpose, one Nepali example and one weakness. For the levels, make a table of who initiates and what can be completed, then say that level five is proactive because the state already holds the data."},{"n":3,"name":"E-Government Infrastructure Development","hook":"A ward gets a new water tank, and every tap in the village still runs dry by March.","why":"Bought layer by layer, infrastructure becomes a set of disconnected purchases: a portal with no bandwidth behind it, a data centre with no backup power, two registries that cannot exchange a name. The citizen becomes the messenger, carrying a certificate between offices, and each failure gets blamed on whichever layer is visible.","story":"A ward builds a drinking-water scheme. The spring is the source, the tank on the ridge stores what the spring gives, the pipes carry it down, and the taps are where people meet the service. Each layer can only promise what the layer above it delivers: a bigger tank does not help a dry spring, and a polished tap does not help a leaking main. Now let every neighbourhood lay its own pipe at its own diameter. Water exists, tanks exist, taps exist, and nothing joins, so families carry buckets between them. The survey before digging, of the spring flow in Chaitra and the users committee rules, is what readiness means.","idea":"Infrastructure is layered, and interoperability is the socket between the layers: network carries traffic, computing and the data centre run the applications, and the architecture layers sit above them. Each layer can only be as reliable as the one beneath it, so e-readiness is an honest audit of the weakest link. Without shared identifiers, schemas and APIs each ministry lays its own pipe, so the citizen becomes the messenger between offices.","steps":[{"t":"Measure the source","d":"Start with connectivity and bandwidth, because every later layer is a promise about how much data can move and how often."},{"t":"Build the tank","d":"Compute, storage and the data centre hold the state's records, so power, cooling and a disaster-recovery site belong in the design, not in facilities trivia."},{"t":"Agree one pipe gauge","d":"Standards, identifiers, schemas and APIs form the interoperability framework. Without them, two departments can hold the same citizen and never meet."},{"t":"Lay taps where people stand","d":"Portals, kiosks and common service centres decide who is truly served; the last mile is where the digital divide lives."},{"t":"Survey before digging","d":"E-readiness tests infrastructure, affordability, skills, legal framework and institutional coordination, and tells you which layer to repair first."}],"oneLiner":"Infrastructure is a supply chain: a service can only be as good as the weakest layer under it.","exam":"For the infrastructure question, list the layers from network up to access and say what each supplies to the one above. Then define interoperability as the agreed socket between them, and close with e-readiness as an audit of the weakest layer, naming EGDI as the country-level version."},{"n":4,"name":"Security for E-Government","hook":"A thief who steals cash is caught by the missing cash; a thief who edits a land record leaves everything looking correct.","why":"If a government treats security as a firewall around a website, it guards the wrong asset. The state's asset is the truth of its records, and the attacker's prize is the state's own signature on a false identity or a false title. That breach is silent: nothing is missing, the balance is right, and only an audit trail shows the edit.","story":"Sit for an hour in a Land Revenue office. The guard checks you at the gate, the clerk finds your file by name, and two officers must sign before a plot changes hands, because one signature is a business and two is a check. The register is the valuable thing in the building, not the cash box: a copied entry moves a real field. So a copy goes to the district headquarters each month, every correction is noted with who made it, and once a year someone compares the copies. A shop thief takes the money; here the thief takes the state's word for it.","idea":"Government security protects authenticity and attribution as much as secrecy, because a forged state record is worth more than the bribe that buys it. Controls therefore stack: physical, network, host, application, data, identity, audit, people. Each layer assumes the one above has already failed, and the audit trail is what turns a silent edit into a caught one.","steps":[{"t":"Price the record, not the server","d":"Ask what one false entry is worth: a wrong title, a fake citizenship, a benefit paid to a ghost. That number sets the control budget."},{"t":"Treat the clerk as a suspect","d":"The insider holds legitimate access, so least privilege, separation of duties and background checks matter more than a perimeter firewall."},{"t":"Stack layers so failure is bounded","d":"Physical, network, host, application, data, identity. Defence in depth assumes one layer will fall and stops the rest from falling with it."},{"t":"Keep a copy nobody can edit","d":"Append-only logs, signed records, offsite backups and digital signatures are what let you prove what the register said last year."},{"t":"Rehearse the response","d":"Monitoring, incident response and periodic audit under ISO 27001 turn a policy document into a loop that improves after each failure."}],"oneLiner":"Government security guards the state's word, not its cash: the attacker wants a record that looks true.","exam":"Open with why government differs: the payoff is legitimacy, the insider is a legitimate user, and down-time is a public failure. Then draw the layered architecture with one control per layer, and close with the management cycle, naming defence in depth and ISO 27001."},{"n":5,"name":"Data Warehousing and Data Mining in Government","hook":"A cooperative's day book records today's sale; the closed ledger decides whether the year can be trusted.","why":"Query the live registers and three things go wrong: the answer takes minutes on data that changes underneath, each ministry computes enrolment with its own definition, and last year's figure is gone because the record was corrected in place. The meeting then argues about whose number is right instead of what to do.","story":"A savings cooperative in a bazaar keeps two sets of books. The counter khata records each deposit and loan as it happens, in the current balance, corrected in ink when a member's name is misspelt: it must be fast, exact, and concerned only with now. At the year end the same entries are consolidated into one ledger for the assembly, with savings, loans and fertiliser in separate sections, every branch in the same format, one column per year, and nothing altered after closing. The auditor then reads ten years across branches and finds the family whose loan is written off every winter. The khata could never answer that.","idea":"A warehouse is the closed ledger: subject-oriented, integrated, time-variant, non-volatile. ETL fixes the definitions once at load, so every report counts the same thing. Mining is then disciplined questioning of stable history, finding patterns a till can never show, because the till overwrites yesterday in order to record today. The operational system is current, normalised and volatile, tuned for many small transactions rather than few large queries, so each correction overwrites yesterday; the warehouse fixes definitions once, freezes each period, and serves marts and OLAP, so mining can find patterns the live register cannot.","steps":[{"t":"Split the till from the ledger","d":"OLTP records many small current transactions; OLAP answers few large historical questions. One system cannot do both well, because each design slows the other."},{"t":"Fix definitions at load","d":"ETL extracts, cleans and conforms each source, so a district and a ministry mean the same thing by the same column."},{"t":"Freeze each period","d":"Snapshots and non-volatility are what make a trend defensible: last year's figure cannot change because someone edited a record this morning."},{"t":"Serve marts, not one pile","d":"Data marts, star schemas and cubes give each ministry only its subject, while the warehouse keeps the shared truth underneath."},{"t":"Ask what the book never asked","d":"Mining, through classification, clustering, association and outlier detection, runs on that history, and CRISP-DM insists it is evaluated before deployment."}],"oneLiner":"A warehouse is the closed ledger behind the till: fixed definitions, frozen history, and patterns the till cannot see.","exam":"Define the warehouse with Inmon's four characteristics first, then contrast OLTP and OLAP in a table. For the government part, take census or commodity prices and show one decision each, then name a mining technique and the leakage it detects. Marks come from explaining non-volatile."},{"n":6,"name":"Case Studies","hook":"A mechanic keeps case files so a new noise is matched to an old fault, not to a town.","why":"Read as travelogue, fifteen case studies become fifteen paragraphs that do not transfer. You can recite Bhoomi's state but not the fault it fixed, so an unseen case, or the question asking why Bhoomi is in every textbook, finds you empty. The examiner is testing diagnosis, and a country list cannot be diagnosed.","story":"A mechanic on the Butwal highway keeps a notebook of every repair. He does not write a Bolero, 2019. He writes the symptom, what he tested, what he changed, what it cost, and whether the noise returned in three months. After a few years the notebook is a diagnostic table, and a new vehicle is matched to a fault class rather than to a town. He keeps the failures too, because the repair that did not hold teaches the next one. That is why the same case appears in every textbook: it is the cleanest example of one fault class.","idea":"A case is evidence for one fault class, not a country report. Bhoomi put the record of rights online and printed it at kiosks, so the village official's power to withhold or alter it disappeared; the measured gain was days and bribes, and the limitation was legal validity and connectivity. Read every case on four axes — fault, intervention, evidence, limitation — and the list stops being history: naming those four is what transfers to an unseen case.","steps":[{"t":"Name the fault first","d":"Before the country, say which layer or maturity level was broken: identity, connectivity, records, reconciliation or grievance."},{"t":"Name who lost power","d":"Every successful case removed somebody's discretion. That is the political cost, and the usual reason a copy fails elsewhere."},{"t":"Name the evidence","d":"Records online, days cut, bribes avoided, leakage found. A case without a measured before and after is an advertisement."},{"t":"Name what limited it","d":"Adoption, data quality, legal gaps or vendor lock-in. The limitation is examinable and is rarely written by students."},{"t":"Carry the lesson across","d":"Compare two cases by fault rather than flag: Nagarik App against e-Seva is an integration question, not a geography question."}],"oneLiner":"A case study is a diagnosis with a budget: pick it for the fault it demonstrates, not the country it happened in.","exam":"For short notes, use four lines: fault, intervention, evidence, limitation, and that alone earns full marks on Bhoomi or CARD. For the Nepal question, group initiatives by layer, attach the Electronic Transactions Act to the legal layer, and say which layer is still weakest."}],"ncc":[{"n":1,"name":"Language Preliminaries","hook":"Why can the same method call print two different names, depending on how the variable was declared?","why":"A language that runs only on one machine and in one author's head cannot be handed to anyone else. If every machine had to understand source, porting would break; if every decision were left to run time, a typo would survive until a user found it. And if declared type and real object were treated as one, a call would hit the wrong code with no error at all.","story":"Think of a ward office in Kathmandu. Every application arrives on the same national form, so any ward can read it; the clerk at the counter still has to translate it into local practice on the day it is processed, once per applicant rather than once per line. Two kinds of record follow. A receipt copies the details onto your own paper — edit your copy and the register never notices. A file number is only a pointer to the master file; two receipts can carry the same number, and one edit is seen by both. Promotion works the same way: a letter addressed to the post goes to whoever holds it today, while a letter stamped to a named desk waits for that person even after transfer.","idea":"C# is compiled twice. Roslyn turns source into MSIL plus metadata inside an assembly, and the CLR JIT-compiles each method to native code on its first call, then caches it. Value types copy their data inline; reference types hold a heap reference. A virtual call with override resolves by the object's real type at run time, while new binds by the declared type at compile time.","steps":[{"t":"Compile to a standard form","d":"Roslyn emits MSIL and metadata into a .dll or .exe; no C# remains and nothing has run yet."},{"t":"Load, verify, JIT","d":"The CLR checks the assembly, then translates each method to native code the first time it is called."},{"t":"Decide where data lives","d":"Structs and primitives are copied inline; classes and arrays sit on the heap behind a reference the GC follows."},{"t":"Address the post, not the person","d":"The binding, not the object, decides which body runs: a virtual call reads the object's actual type at run time; a non-virtual or new call is fixed by the declared type."},{"t":"Sign a contract, not a lineage","d":"An abstract class shares code down one line of inheritance; an interface is a can-do promise any number of classes can sign."},{"t":"Leave a call-back slip","d":"A delegate holds a method reference; an event is that delegate exposed safely so only the owner can raise it."}],"oneLiner":"C# compiles once to a portable form, then to native code per machine; only virtual calls wait to see the real object.","exam":"For the 10-mark compilation question, draw source, Roslyn, MSIL plus metadata, assembly, CLR, JIT, native code in that order. Earn the rest by contrasting override with new in a few lines of code and naming exactly what decides each one."},{"n":2,"name":"Introduction to ASP.NET","hook":"Why does the same web application need a different installer on Windows, on Linux and on a phone?","why":"A framework welded to one operating system forces every later decision to follow it: the server, the licence, the update cycle. A stack that ships as one indivisible block cannot reuse a small piece without dragging the rest along, and a machine-wide runtime means two applications on one machine cannot disagree about the version they need. The web layer inherits all three limits.","story":"Think of a bus company that began with a single route inside one city. Its buses were built for those roads, serviced at one depot, and every passenger shared whatever engine the depot had installed that year. Expanding meant building the same design to a different gauge, and an old bus could not run beside a new one on the same road. The answer was a bus any country's roads accept, assembled from parts bought per trip, with one agreed socket size so a part from any workshop fits any chassis. They kept the old body styles but moved them onto the new chassis, and put a single ticket counter in front of the whole fleet.","idea":".NET Framework is the Windows-only runtime the old web stack assumed. .NET Core and .NET 5+ are cross-platform, open source and modular: the runtime and libraries arrive as NuGet packages with the application, so versions sit side by side. Mono is a separate implementation for other platforms, .NET Standard a contract of shared APIs, and ASP.NET Core one chassis carrying MVC, Web API, Razor Pages and SignalR. Because the runtime is resolved per application rather than installed once for the machine, each project restores its framework and the host loads it inside that application's own process, where a machine-wide framework would force every site onto one version and one update cycle.","steps":[{"t":"Separate runtime from app","d":"The framework is restored as packages per project, so the app carries the version it was compiled against."},{"t":"Target a contract","d":".NET Standard lists the APIs every implementation must supply, which is how one library serves Framework, Core and Mono."},{"t":"Pick the body style","d":"Web Forms binds events and ViewState, MVC separates concerns, Web API returns data, and Core carries all of them."},{"t":"Drive it from one CLI","d":"dotnet new, restore, build, run, test and publish cover the lifecycle without opening an IDE."},{"t":"Know the folder map","d":"Program.cs builds the host, the csproj lists packages, wwwroot holds static files, and Controllers, Views and Models hold the app."}],"oneLiner":"ASP.NET Core is a cross-platform runtime plus per-project packages, not a Windows feature, and one chassis carries every web style.","exam":"For the comparison question, table Framework against Core on platform, deployment, packaging and versioning, then place Mono and .NET Standard in one line each. For the practical question, list the dotnet commands in lifecycle order and name the file or folder each one touches."},{"n":3,"name":"HTTP and ASP.NET Core","hook":"Every request enters by the same door, yet a stylesheet and a login page are handled by different guards.","why":"HTTP forgets: each request arrives alone, with no thread tying it to the last one. A server with no fixed order turns every feature into a special case — static files get checked for login, errors leak stack traces, and redirect loops appear because nobody agreed where HTTPS is enforced. The order of the checkpoints is the design, not an implementation detail.","story":"Picture a restaurant with one entrance and a line of staff just inside it. The doorman turns away anyone not on the secure side, the guard checks identity, the usher checks whether your ticket covers this hall, and only then does the maître d' read your table number and send the order to the right station. Each of them can end your visit on the spot, and the ones nearest the door end it cheapest. A request for the menu card is settled at the shelf by the door and never reaches the kitchen at all. The waiter writes your order on a ticket, hands it in, and forgets you completely until you call again.","idea":"Kestrel accepts the connection and parses one HTTP message: method, path, headers, optional body. It passes that into a middleware pipeline assembled in Program.cs, where each component may act, pass on, or stop and write a response. Order is the contract: exception handling, HTTPS, static files, routing, authentication, authorization, endpoints. The controller works with the model and returns a view, and the response retraces the same layers.","steps":[{"t":"Parse one message","d":"Kestrel reads the request line, headers and body off the socket; the method and path decide what the message means."},{"t":"Enter the pipeline in order","d":"Each Use component wraps the next, so the first registered runs first going in and last coming out."},{"t":"Short-circuit where allowed","d":"Static files, a redirect or failed authorization can write a response and stop, and nothing downstream ever runs."},{"t":"Resolve the endpoint","d":"Routing matches the path to a controller action and its route values before authorization looks at the request."},{"t":"Run the action","d":"The controller calls the model, gets data, and picks an action result such as View, Json or Redirect."},{"t":"Render and return","d":"Razor turns model plus view into HTML, and the response carries a status line, headers and a body."}],"oneLiner":"A request is one HTTP message walked through an ordered pipeline, and the first layer that answers ends the journey.","exam":"Draw the pipeline left to right and label where static files, authentication and authorization sit; that diagram alone carries half the 10 marks. Then trace one GET for a stylesheet and one for a protected page, saying which layer stops each and why."},{"n":4,"name":"Creating ASP.NET Core MVC Applications","hook":"A form arrives with three fields missing. Who was supposed to notice, the browser or the server?","why":"An action that reads raw query strings, builds HTML by hand and opens its own database connection is untestable and unsafe. Every page repeats the same parsing, a missing field reaches the database as null, and two developers cannot touch one screen without colliding. The framework exists to make the boring parts automatic and the untrusted parts checked before your code runs.","story":"Think of a hospital outpatient department. Reception reads your card and sends you to the right room; the doctor asks the questions on a standard form, and the form refuses to be filed while a required box is empty. What you leave with depends on the diagnosis: a prescription, a lab slip, a referral, or a polite not today. Supplies never come from the doctor's own cupboard — a tray is issued for your visit and returned, a fresh needle comes out for each use, and the autoclave in the basement is the single one everybody shares. The nurse writes on the hospital's printed pad, never on loose paper.","idea":"Routing maps a URL to a controller action, model binding fills action parameters, and data annotations decide whether ModelState is valid before your code runs. The action then chooses an action result: View renders Razor, Json returns data, RedirectToAction sends the browser elsewhere. That redirect exists because the browser's last request must not be a POST: returning the view leaves the address bar on the submitted URL, so refresh or back repeats the form and inserts a duplicate row, while a 302 and a GET leave an idempotent request that can be repeated harmlessly.","steps":[{"t":"Match the route","d":"Conventional templates use controller, action and optional id; attribute routes put the pattern on the action or the controller."},{"t":"Bind the inputs","d":"Parameters are filled by name and type from route values, query string, form fields, a JSON body or headers."},{"t":"Validate before acting","d":"Data annotations and binding produce ModelState; an invalid model returns the same view carrying the messages."},{"t":"Choose a result","d":"View, PartialView, Json, File, RedirectToAction, NotFound and StatusCode each write a different response shape. A successful POST returns RedirectToAction, never View: the browser's last request must stay a GET, or refresh and back resend the form and insert a duplicate row. The answer is a 302 to a GET on a safe URL, idempotent, so repeating it is harmless."},{"t":"Render with Razor","d":"The view is compiled C# over HTML, and tag helpers turn asp-for and asp-action into correct names, ids and URLs."},{"t":"Resolve by lifetime","d":"Scoped gives one per request, transient a new one per resolve, singleton one per process; a captive singleton is the classic bug."}],"oneLiner":"An action is a route, a bound model, a validity check and a chosen result, with its collaborators injected rather than built.","exam":"The 10-mark answer walks one POST: route, binding, ModelState, action result, then the Razor view. Add the tag-helper form and the three DI lifetimes with one line each, and say which lifetime a DbContext needs and why."},{"n":5,"name":"Working with Database","hook":"Two programs read the same table; one breaks when a column is renamed and the other does not. Why?","why":"Every hand-written query embeds the schema in a string, so a renamed column becomes a run-time crash in a file nobody was editing. String concatenation turns user input into SQL, which is the injection hole. And with no record of what changed, saving means re-sending whole rows and hoping nobody else wrote in between. Objects and tables speak different languages, and someone has to translate.","story":"Think of a Nepali office that must file everything in an English-language registry. One clerk translates each letter by hand, word for word; he is fast and exact, but the day the registry renames a form, every letter he ever wrote has to be rewritten. The other clerk listens to what you want, writes the letter himself, and keeps a running note of everything you have changed since you last spoke, then sends one batch when you say you are done. He also keeps a history of how the registry was rearranged, so any branch can be brought to the same layout. You still have to name the shelves, or he fetches one row at a time.","idea":"ADO.NET is the hand translator: SqlConnection opens, SqlCommand carries SQL and parameters, ExecuteReader streams rows forward-only, and an adapter fills a disconnected DataSet. EF Core is the clerk: a DbSet is a table, LINQ is the request, and the DbContext tracks each loaded entity so SaveChanges writes only the differences. Either way parameters keep code and data on separate channels: the statement text is parsed and its plan built before any value is bound, so a value can only ever occupy a slot and never become syntax. Manual escaping or stripping is a blocklist that misses numeric contexts, second-order data and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table.","steps":[{"t":"Open one connection","d":"SqlConnection holds the session; open late and close early, and let using dispose it even when an exception fires."},{"t":"Keep data as data","d":"Parameters bind values after the SQL is parsed, so a quote typed by a user can never become syntax. Manual escaping is a blocklist: it misses numeric positions, data re-read from the database, and encoding differences, so one forgotten path still exposes the table."},{"t":"Choose the read shape","d":"ExecuteNonQuery returns affected rows, ExecuteScalar one value, ExecuteReader a stream, an adapter a filled DataSet."},{"t":"Map rows to objects","d":"EF Core turns LINQ into SQL, and Include or ThenInclude fetches related rows eagerly instead of once per parent."},{"t":"Track, then commit","d":"The context keeps original and current values, so SaveChanges emits only changed columns inside a transaction."},{"t":"Record the schema","d":"dotnet ef migrations add writes the change as code and database update applies it, so no environment drifts."}],"oneLiner":"ADO.NET makes you write the SQL, while EF Core writes it from LINQ and remembers what you changed until SaveChanges.","exam":"Answer both halves: an ADO.NET snippet with connection, parameterised command and reader, then a DbContext with CRUD calls. Marks come from naming ExecuteReader against ExecuteScalar, and from explaining change tracking and how ToList materialises a deferred query."},{"n":6,"name":"State Management","hook":"You log in, click one link, and the site asks you to log in again. What was never stored?","why":"HTTP keeps no memory between requests, so anything spanning two pages must be written down somewhere and read back. Guess wrong and the app either loses the user's basket on every click, or stores a password in a cookie the browser can edit. The real question is never which technique to use, but who owns this memory and how long it may live.","story":"Think of a bank where the clerk at the window serves whoever is standing there and remembers nothing once you step away. To be served as the same customer twice, something must carry your identity. You can keep a slip in your own pocket with your name on it — convenient, but anyone who reads your pocket can copy it, and the clerk must still verify it. Or the bank keeps a file in the back room and hands you only a number; the file stays inside and the number means nothing alone. A note passed from one counter to the next is read once and shredded, and the notice board in the hall is shared by everyone until someone takes the old paper down.","idea":"Cookies live in the browser and travel with every request, so they are small, visible and tamperable unless protected. Session keeps the data on the server and sends only an id cookie, so it escapes the size limit but needs a store. TempData carries one message across one redirect and is read once. ViewData lasts a single request; IMemoryCache is shared by all users until it expires or is removed.","steps":[{"t":"Ask who needs it","d":"One request, one user, one redirect, or the whole application decides the scope before the storage."},{"t":"Keep secrets server-side","d":"Session writes to a cache or database and the browser holds only the session id, so a copied cookie is not the data."},{"t":"Cross one redirect","d":"TempData is read once then discarded; Peek reads without consuming, and Keep extends it one more hop."},{"t":"Share deliberately","d":"IMemoryCache serves every user, so keys must include the user or tenant and every write needs an invalidation plan."},{"t":"Harden the cookie","d":"HttpOnly blocks script access, Secure keeps it on HTTPS, and SameSite stops it riding along on another site's request."}],"oneLiner":"State is the memory HTTP refuses to keep, so you choose an owner, a lifetime and a trust boundary for every piece of it.","exam":"Compare server-side and client-side in a table with scope, storage, size and risk columns, then justify one choice for a cart, a flash message and a theme. Extra marks come from naming HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite on cookies, and Peek and Keep on TempData."},{"n":7,"name":"Client-side Development","hook":"The form was validated before it was sent, and the server still rejected it. Which side was lying?","why":"A page that reloads for every click feels broken, and a page that trusts its own script is broken. The browser is a runtime the user fully controls: the script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a disabled script, an edited form or a direct request reaches the action with any values at all. Push too little work there and every keystroke costs a round trip; push too much and the server stops being the authority. This unit is about drawing that line.","story":"Think of a company that opens a small branch inside the customer's own building. The branch answers simple questions on the spot — is this form complete, which page comes next — without phoning head office, which is why the customer is not kept waiting. But the branch is staffed by whoever walks in, so head office re-checks every form that arrives, however confident the branch sounded. Some branches keep the whole catalogue locally and phone only for facts, so moving between shelves needs no call at all. Before opening, someone packs the day's stationery into one box so the branch is not unpacking a hundred parcels at the door.","idea":"Client-side code runs in the browser and can be read, skipped or replaced, so it buys speed, never authority: the browser is a runtime the visitor controls and cannot grant authority. The script is downloaded, readable and disposable, so a direct request, a disabled script or an edited form reaches the action with any values at all. ModelState re-runs the same annotations server-side before the database is touched, which is why client rules only save round trips. jQuery with unobtrusive validation mirrors those rules for instant feedback, AJAX exchanges JSON without a reload, and a SPA renders and routes in the browser and calls a Web API.","steps":[{"t":"Render once, then patch","d":"The server sends a shell or a full page, and script updates parts of the DOM from JSON instead of reloading."},{"t":"Validate twice, trust once","d":"Data annotations become HTML attributes for the browser, and the same attributes run again in ModelState on the server before the database is touched. Client rules therefore only save round trips; they never grant authority."},{"t":"Call the API, not the page","d":"jQuery or fetch posts to a route returning JSON, and the callback decides what to insert or which error to show."},{"t":"Move routing to the browser","d":"An SPA intercepts links, renders a component per URL, and asks the API only for data."},{"t":"Ship it as one bundle","d":"A bundler resolves imports and minifies, and TypeScript's types are erased before the browser ever sees the file."}],"oneLiner":"The browser is a second runtime that may be fast but must never be trusted; every check it does, the server repeats.","exam":"For the validation question say plainly that client rules are user experience and server rules are security, then show the annotation, the tag helper and the ModelState check together. For SPA, compare Angular, React and Vue on structure and data flow, and place the Web API behind them."},{"n":8,"name":"Securing ASP.NET Core","hook":"A user is logged in and still gets a 403. What is the difference between that and a 401?","why":"Every attack here begins the same way: the server treats something the client sent as trustworthy. A missing identity check lets anyone in; a missing permission check lets a logged-in user read someone else's record; an unencoded comment or an unparameterised query turns ordinary input into code. None of these fail loudly — the application keeps working perfectly while it leaks.","story":"Think of an office building with one gate and many doors. At the gate you show a citizenship certificate; the guard does not keep the document, only a fingerprint of it, and compares that against the record. This answers who you are and nothing more. Inside, each door carries its own list: this floor is for finance, this room only for the person named on the file. A delivery boy who reaches the lift with your name on the parcel still cannot open your door. Every form you sign is torn along a perforation and the counterfoil kept at the gate, so a letter typed in your name by someone else arrives with the wrong half.","idea":"Authentication establishes identity: Identity hashes passwords with PBKDF2, stores users, roles and claims, and issues a cookie or token. Authorization then decides access through [Authorize], roles, policies or claims, and runs after authentication in the pipeline. Anti-forgery pairs a cookie with a form token. Razor encodes output, parameters keep data out of SQL syntax, and Url.IsLocalUrl blocks an off-site returnUrl. That order is not optional: authorization can only judge a principal that already exists, so reversed it would meet an anonymous principal, every protected action would challenge with 401, and no role check could ever pass.","steps":[{"t":"Verify identity once","d":"The submitted password is hashed and compared server-side; success issues an encrypted cookie carrying the user's claims."},{"t":"Read the claims","d":"Roles and claims travel with each request, so authorization needs no extra database lookup per door. Authentication runs first because authorization can only judge a principal that already exists; reversed, every protected action meets an anonymous user and challenges with 401."},{"t":"Gate the door, not the lobby","d":"Put [Authorize] on the controller, [AllowAnonymous] only where intended, and a role or policy on narrower actions."},{"t":"Prove the form is yours","d":"The anti-forgery cookie and hidden field must match, which a page served from another origin cannot produce."},{"t":"Encode before display","d":"Razor escapes model values by default; Html.Raw is a deliberate, audited exception rather than a convenience."},{"t":"Check the destination","d":"Redirect only to a local URL, so a crafted returnUrl cannot walk the user to another site after login."}],"oneLiner":"Authentication says who you are, authorization says which doors open, and every attack is the server trusting the client once too often.","exam":"Start with the 401 against 403 distinction, then show AddIdentity, UseAuthentication before UseAuthorization, and one [Authorize(Roles)] example. For the attack question give XSS, SQL injection and CSRF each as input, mechanism and fix — the fix is where the marks sit."},{"n":9,"name":"Hosting and Deployment","hook":"The application runs on your laptop and dies on the server: same code, different front door.","why":"A working build is not a deployed application. Something must own the socket, terminate TLS, survive a restart, and read the production connection string from somewhere other than a file in the repository. Skip that and the app refuses to start, serves plain HTTP behind a proxy that already decrypted the request, or ships development secrets inside the image.","story":"Think of a shop that must open in three different places. The shopkeeper can stand at the street door and serve customers directly — fast, and the same shopkeeper works in any city. In a mall, the reception desk handles the signboard, the security and the queue, then passes people through to the shop; it can also split the crowd across two identical shops. The same shop can be packed into a standard shipping container with its counter, lights and stock inside, so it opens identically on any dock, and the packing list deliberately leaves the workshop and the tools behind. Renting space in a managed mall means someone else owns the building, the power and the repairs.","idea":"Kestrel is the cross-platform web server that owns the socket; IIS, Nginx or Apache usually sit in front as a reverse proxy for TLS, compression and load balancing. In-process hosting runs the app inside the IIS worker process, while out-of-process has the ASP.NET Core Module forward requests to a separate Kestrel. A multi-stage Dockerfile compiles on the SDK image and copies only the published output onto the smaller runtime image. Because the proxy forwards rather than executes, it terminates TLS and passes plain HTTP to a Kestrel process listening on localhost, so forwarded headers must be honoured and TLS must be terminated in exactly one place.","steps":[{"t":"Publish, do not just build","d":"dotnet publish in Release resolves packages and writes a folder to run, not a project to compile."},{"t":"Let Kestrel own the socket","d":"The app listens on a configured port and, behind a proxy, trusts forwarded headers for scheme and client address."},{"t":"Put a proxy in front","d":"Nginx or IIS terminates TLS, serves static files and balances instances, then forwards plain HTTP to Kestrel. That is why TLS is terminated at exactly one place and forwarded headers must tell the app the original scheme and client address."},{"t":"Choose in or out of process","d":"In-process loads the app inside the IIS worker for speed; out-of-process keeps Kestrel separate and bridges through the module."},{"t":"Pack one artifact","d":"The SDK stage compiles and publishes, the runtime stage copies only the output, so the image holds no compiler or source."},{"t":"Configure per environment","d":"Environment variables override appsettings, secrets come from a vault, and migrations run before the new version takes traffic."}],"oneLiner":"Deployment is deciding who owns the socket and where configuration lives: Kestrel serves, a proxy fronts it, a container carries it.","exam":"Draw internet, proxy, Kestrel and app for both hosting models and mark where TLS ends. Then give the two Dockerfile stages and the production checklist — environment, secrets, HTTPS, health check, migrations — as short labelled lines."}],"tw":[{"n":1,"name":"What Is Technical Writing","hook":"A water pump bought in Asan comes with a glossy brochure, and a folded sheet with a diagram and three warnings.","why":"Without the folded sheet the buyer guesses which pipe is the inlet, runs the motor dry and calls the shop. Writing that is charming but unusable does the same thing: it spends the reader's attention and returns nothing. Technical writing exists because someone has to act correctly after reading, not admire the prose.","story":"The pump shop in Asan. The brochure says the pump is powerful and elegant; the folded sheet says half a horsepower, inlet twenty-five millimetres, prime before starting, never run dry. One is written for the buyer's mood, the other for the buyer's hands. The shopkeeper who includes the sheet gets fewer return calls, fewer burnt motors and fewer refunds, because the reader can finish the job alone. That sheet names its reader, fixes one purpose and can be checked — does the pump run? Everything in this paper is that sheet at a larger scale.","idea":"Technical writing is writing that changes what a specific reader can do, so its success test is behavioural, not aesthetic. Its characteristics follow from that: clarity removes ambiguity about who does what, accuracy keeps a verified number usable, and organisation lets a scanning reader reach the step. The consequence is measurable, since a correct installation with no support call proves the document worked; creative writing is judged by how it makes you feel, technical writing by whether the task gets done.","steps":[{"t":"Name the reader","d":"Decide who must act on this and what they already know, because that sets every word after it."},{"t":"Fix one purpose","d":"Inform, instruct or persuade. A document that attempts all three at once usually fails at each."},{"t":"Cut what does not help","d":"A sentence that does not help the reader act is still a sentence they must read."},{"t":"Make it checkable","d":"Give units, steps and warnings the reader can verify against the machine in front of them."},{"t":"Judge by the action","d":"Success is a working pump and no phone call, not a compliment on the writing."}],"oneLiner":"Technical writing is judged by what the reader can do afterwards, not by how good the prose sounds.","exam":"Define it in one line, then give the characteristics as consequences of that definition rather than a memorized list. For the comparison question, draw four rows — technical, creative, academic, journalistic — and contrast purpose, audience, tone and success test."},{"n":2,"name":"Audience and Purpose","hook":"One notice about road widening is pinned at the ward office, and five people read it needing five different things.","why":"A writer who imagines 'the public' writes for nobody. The resident wants to know if her wall goes, the engineer wants the chainage, the journalist wants a quote, the secretary wants a date. A document that answers none of them gets ignored or misread, and the writer blames the readers. A document aimed at everyone drifts toward the average and fails the one reader who must act.","story":"The ward office notice board. One road-widening notice must reach the secretary who signs the file, the engineer who sets the chainage, the shopkeeper whose shutter will move, and a journalist passing the board. The secretary needs the decision and the date on one page; the engineer needs measurements; the shopkeeper needs to know when, and how much compensation; the journalist needs who decided. Written for the secretary alone, the shopkeeper panics. Written for everyone at once, nobody finishes it. So the writer builds the notice around the reader who must act, then adds what the others need in a table, an annex or one extra line.","idea":"Audience analysis is deciding which reader the document is built around. The primary reader acts on it, secondary readers approve or pass it on, tertiary readers meet it by accident. Their knowledge, role, attitude and where they read it decide your vocabulary, your depth and your order of information. Purpose is the single verb the document must accomplish.","steps":[{"t":"Find the primary reader","d":"The person who must act or decide, not the largest group who might read it."},{"t":"Map the secondary readers","d":"Approvers and passers-on get what they need, without being allowed to dilute the main text."},{"t":"Ask what they know","d":"Expert, technician or layperson decides whether you define a term or simply use it."},{"t":"Fix one purpose","d":"One verb for the document; anything serving a different purpose is cut or moved to an annex."},{"t":"Match the medium","d":"Urgent goes to a call or SMS, permanent goes to paper, complex goes to a page with headings."}],"oneLiner":"Write for the one reader who must act, then add what the others need without rewriting it for them.","exam":"Answer with the three audience types, then the six factors — knowledge, role, needs, attitude, context, constraints — each with one line of consequence. Finish with a worked example, such as a seed-sowing guide for farmers, and say precisely what changes because of that analysis."},{"n":3,"name":"The Writing Process","hook":"A mason builds a boundary wall in one pass and finds it leaning at the far end, with the cement already set.","why":"Writing in one pass feels efficient and is the most expensive way to work. You polish sentences in a document whose structure is wrong, you discover on page six that the reader wanted something else, and the spelling check happens last, when you are tired and least able to see your own mistakes.","story":"The boundary wall: first the mason sets the string line and counts bricks; that is planning, and it decides everything after. Then he lays bricks quickly without stopping to polish each joint, because stopping kills the rhythm; that is drafting. Then he holds a plumb to the corner, finds the third course out of line and pulls it down; that is revising, and it sends him back to the string line. Only then does he point the joints and clean the face, which is copyediting. The owner walks the wall and signs, which is publishing. Pointing joints on a wall about to be pulled down is wasted work, and so is perfect grammar on a section that should not exist.","idea":"The process separates thinking from wording from polishing, because each needs a different kind of attention and doing all three at once does none well. Planning decides what the document is, drafting produces material without judging it, revising judges structure and content, copyediting fixes the surface, publishing puts it in the reader's hands. It loops, because a revision can send you back to planning.","steps":[{"t":"Plan before prose","d":"Audience, purpose, research, outline, medium and schedule, all settled before a sentence is written."},{"t":"Draft without stopping","d":"Write fast and leave gaps; judging while writing kills the flow and hides the missing argument."},{"t":"Revise the whole","d":"Check content, order and completeness against the purpose, and be willing to cut a section outright."},{"t":"Edit the surface","d":"Grammar, punctuation, terminology and consistency, once the structure has stopped moving."},{"t":"Publish and collect","d":"Format, proof, distribute, archive, and read the feedback you get back."}],"oneLiner":"Draft fast, revise the structure, then polish the words — never all three at the same time.","exam":"Name the five stages in order, then say what each one decides, which turns a list into an answer. For collaborative writing, add roles, one style sheet and a single editor for voice, plus two advantages and two costs. Recursion — revision looping back to planning — separates a good answer from a recited one."},{"n":4,"name":"Brief Correspondence","hook":"An email with the subject line 'Query' sits unopened in a bank manager's inbox behind forty others.","why":"Correspondence is the only writing where the reader has not agreed to read you. They are mid-task, holding a phone, deciding in three seconds whether you are work or noise. A vague subject, a buried request and a message that repeats the whole thread spend the one thing you cannot buy back: the moment they chose to open it.","story":"Walking into a bank manager's room at noon, you knock, and the knock itself says who you are and why. You state your business in the first sentence, because four people are behind you. You hand over one page, not the whole file, and you leave knowing what he will do next. An email is that visit: the subject is the knock, the first sentence is the business, the body is the one page, the close is the agreed next step. A memo is the same visit inside the building, where no knock is needed, so the header says who wrote it, to whom, when and about what. A letter is the visit that must survive the journey and the file, so it carries letterhead, inside address, salutation and signature.","idea":"Correspondence is built for a reader who is still deciding whether to give you their attention: the subject names the business, the first line states the purpose, the middle carries only what is needed to decide or act, and the close says who does what next. The distance decides the ceremony. A memo stays inside one organisation, so it needs no salutation, no inside address and no complimentary close, only TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT; a letter may travel to a customer, supplier or authority, so it must identify both parties, carry a signature and survive being filed.","steps":[{"t":"Make the subject work","d":"Six to eight words naming the matter and the action, so it can be triaged from a list."},{"t":"State purpose first","d":"One sentence telling the reader whether this concerns them at all."},{"t":"One idea per paragraph","d":"Short blocks the reader can answer point by point instead of re-reading one wall of text."},{"t":"Close with the next step","d":"Who does what by when, so that silence is never ambiguous."},{"t":"Choose the ceremony","d":"Memo inside, letter outside, email for speed; the distance decides the formality. A memo therefore drops the salutation, inside address and complimentary close, keeping TO, FROM, DATE and SUBJECT; a letter keeps them because it must identify both parties and survive being filed."}],"oneLiner":"Every message is a knock on a busy person's door: say who you are, what you want, and by when.","exam":"For the comparison question draw three columns — email, memo, letter — and contrast audience, header, salutation, close and typical use. Then label a memo's TO, FROM, DATE, SUBJECT and a letter's nine parts in order. The labelled diagram carries more marks than a paragraph describing it."},{"n":5,"name":"Document Design and Graphics","hook":"A patient at the hospital gate cannot read the board, and still finds the X-ray room in ten seconds.","why":"Readers do not read documents, they navigate them. A page of unbroken text with no headings makes a reader hunt for the one number they came for, and a graph with an unlabelled axis makes them guess. The information is present and unreachable, which for anyone in a hurry is the same as absent.","story":"A hospital outpatient block. Nobody arrives calm and nobody reads the whole signboard; they look for one word. So the block is built for the scan: a corridor wide enough for two people to pass, one board per direction, the same font and colour on every board so a board is recognised before it is read, and the emergency arrow in the one colour nothing else uses. The floor map at the entrance does a job no list of room numbers could. A document is that block: margins are corridor width, headings are the boards, a table is the directory, a chart is the map. Design is not decoration; it is what lets a frightened reader arrive.","idea":"Design is signposting for a reader under pressure, who goes to the figure first and may never read the paragraph that explains it. Proximity groups what belongs together, alignment puts things on a shared line, repetition teaches the reader your pattern once, and contrast says what matters first. A graphic needs titles, axis labels with units, a legend and a source so it can be understood in isolation, and those also stop a truncated axis from exaggerating a difference; without them the reader guesses, and a guess in a procedure or a budget is a defect.","steps":[{"t":"Group by proximity","d":"Related items sit close; white space is what separates one topic from the next."},{"t":"Align everything","d":"One invisible line per column, so the eye stops hunting for where things start."},{"t":"Repeat the pattern","d":"Same heading style, colours and table shape, so the reader learns the page only once."},{"t":"Create one contrast","d":"One size, weight or colour for the point that must be seen before anything else."},{"t":"Label every graphic","d":"Title, axes, units, legend and source, so it survives being read on its own."}],"oneLiner":"Design for the reader who scans: group, align, repeat, contrast, and label every graphic so it stands alone.","exam":"Name the four principles — proximity, alignment, repetition, contrast — and give one concrete effect of each rather than a definition. For graphics, match chart to data in a small table: line for trend, bar for comparison, pie for parts of a whole, table for exact values. Then list the labelling checklist."},{"n":6,"name":"Writing for the Web","hook":"A bus stops for forty seconds at a highway tea shop, and the shop has forty seconds to be understood.","why":"On paper a reader has committed. On the web they arrived from a search result, are still deciding, and leave in seconds at no cost to themselves. A page that opens with the company's history, splits one answer across six screens and hides the price in a PDF loses them before the first useful sentence, and you never hear about it.","story":"The tea shop at the highway stop. The bus idles for forty seconds and the passenger is standing, holding a bag, deciding. So the shop does not tell the story of its founder. It puts the flask on the counter, the price on a board at eye level and the change ready, in that order, because that is the order the passenger needs them. The menu is chunked into tea, snacks and cold drinks so that one glance answers one question, and the passenger who wants nothing but water is not made to read the whole board. Leave the answer at the back and the bus goes without you.","idea":"Web readers scan for relevance before they commit to reading, so the page answers at the top and expands downwards. The inverted pyramid puts the conclusion first, chunking splits it into one-idea blocks, and headings, bullets and front-loaded keywords make those blocks findable. One page does one task, because a page that does three gives the visitor three chances to leave. The visitor arrives from a search result, mid-task, and leaves at no cost, so attention must be earned in the first screen, and a page that builds to its answer spends the reader's patience before delivering anything.","steps":[{"t":"Answer at the top","d":"Conclusion first; supporting detail is for the reader who chooses to stay."},{"t":"Chunk one idea at a time","d":"Short sections, each answering a question the visitor actually arrived with."},{"t":"Front-load the keywords","d":"Keywords first in headings, links and sentences, because that is all a scanner reads."},{"t":"Make links descriptive","d":"The link text says where it goes; 'click here' tells the reader nothing about the destination."},{"t":"Design for one hand","d":"Mobile-first, fast, no horizontal scroll, because that is the screen the visitor is holding."}],"oneLiner":"On the web you have seconds: put the answer first, one idea per chunk, and make every heading scannable.","exam":"Open with the one difference — web readers scan and leave — and then convert it into rules: inverted pyramid, chunking, scannability, front-loaded keywords. For the design question add navigation, breadcrumbs, search, footer and accessibility. A before-and-after link example, 'click here' against 'download the 2082 syllabus', earns the last mark."},{"n":7,"name":"Information Reports","hook":"The health post sends the district office a monthly sheet of numbers, with no opinion attached.","why":"An organisation cannot act on what it has not recorded, and cannot compare months described differently. Without a fixed reporting shape every report is a fresh essay: the district cannot total two health posts, a missing month looks like a good month, and the writer answers the same questions again by phone.","story":"In a hill health post, on the last day of the month, the nurse fills in the same form as last month: patients seen, vaccines given, stock-outs, referrals. She writes what happened and stops there, because 'we need a new fridge' is the district's decision and a different document. Because the form repeats, twelve months can be laid side by side. The campaign running since Kartik gets a short note: what is finished, what is half done, what slipped. Before the free eye camp the post sends the local FM a release answering who, what, when, where and why in the first sentence. The new vaccine fridge arrives with a description of its parts and how it keeps cold, so a new staffer can run it without a phone call.","idea":"An information report carries facts to a reader who was not present, in a shape that reader can use. Nothing is recommended, because the recommendation belongs to whoever has the authority. If the writer does recommend, the report claims authority it was not given, so it is either rejected or acted on as advice it was never scoped to give. Structure follows the reader's questions: what period, what happened, what is unfinished, what changed the plan. The summary is the plain-language version for the manager; the abstract is the compressed technical version for the specialist.","steps":[{"t":"Fix the shape","d":"The same headings every time, so two reports can be read side by side."},{"t":"Separate fact from comment","d":"Findings stay in the body; opinion appears only where it is labelled as opinion."},{"t":"State period and scope","d":"Which dates, which site, and what is deliberately left out."},{"t":"Show completion honestly","d":"Percentage done, what slipped and why, before anyone has to ask."},{"t":"Let the summary stand alone","d":"A reader who stops after the summary must still have the truth."}],"oneLiner":"An information report carries facts, not advice, in a shape that lets an absent reader compare and decide.","exam":"Define it against the recommendation report in one sentence: it reports, it does not recommend. Then give periodic sections and progress sections as two labelled lists, and put summary against abstract in a four-row table. A short news release with a dateline and a who-what-when-where-why lead covers the applied part."},{"n":8,"name":"Employment Communication","hook":"Two hundred resumes reach a software company for one post, and the shortlist is made in an afternoon.","why":"A resume that lists everything you have done makes the reader do the judging. They have twenty seconds and one question — can this person do this job — so an autobiography of every school, hobby and unrelated job buries the two lines that would have answered it. The writer loses the interview, not on merit, but on effort demanded.","story":"The recruitment desk at a software company in Jhamsikhel. The guard has twelve names for interview day and behind them sit two hundred files. The reader is not curious about you; she is holding a job description and looking for three things in it. So the resume is not a life story, it is an argument: this is the post, this is the evidence that I have done this work, this is the number that proves it. A fresher argues from projects, a person returning after two years argues by skill, a steady career argues by time. The cover letter is the two sentences at reception: why this company, why me, can we meet.","idea":"Employment writing is an argument addressed to one advertised job. The resume supplies evidence, arranged in whichever order makes the strongest case — time, skill, or both — and every line answers the reader's only question. The cover letter supplies the reason for this employer in particular. Neither is a record of a life; both are a match between a stated need and demonstrated work.","steps":[{"t":"Read the advert first","d":"The advertisement is the marking scheme; the resume answers its lines in its own words."},{"t":"Choose the order","d":"Chronological for a steady career, functional for a gap or change, hybrid for most people."},{"t":"Prove with numbers","d":"Action, task, result: 'cut load time by forty per cent' beats 'responsible for performance'."},{"t":"Cut the personal","d":"Photo and marital status take space from evidence unless the employer asked for them."},{"t":"Write the letter for this firm","d":"Two achievements that fit, one reason for this employer, one clear request for a meeting."}],"oneLiner":"A resume is an argument that you can do this job, evidenced in numbers, not an autobiography.","exam":"Define resume against CV in one line, then give the three formats and the case each suits — steady career, gap or change, most people today. For the letter question use the four-paragraph shape: post applied for, evidence, why this organisation, request for interview. Quantified achievement examples carry the application marks."},{"n":9,"name":"Presentations","hook":"A bus to Pokhara has eleven stops, and the driver who stops everywhere arrives at midnight.","why":"A presentation fails when the speaker tries to say everything, because an audience holds about one idea at a time. Slides packed with paragraphs get read instead of heard, the speaker runs over and skips the conclusion, and the room leaves with eleven half-ideas and no decision. The content was all there; the shape was not.","story":"The Kathmandu to Pokhara bus. Before moving, the conductor says where the bus goes and which stops are coming, which lets a passenger relax. Each stop is one place: everyone gets off, looks at one thing, and gets back on. The bus does not try to show the whole hill from every stop, and between stops the conductor calls the next one, so nobody wonders whether the bus is lost. The schedule is fixed, and a stop that runs long is a stop that eats the next one. Arrival is the conclusion: everyone off, bags in hand, knowing what to do now.","idea":"A presentation is a queue of one idea per slide, held together by signposting. The introduction buys attention and sets the route, the body delivers one point at a time with a spoken transition between, and the conclusion repeats the point and asks for the action. Slides carry evidence, not script, because an audience can read or listen but not both. An audience holds about one idea at a time, so eleven half-ideas leave the room with none, and a slide of paragraph text is read instead of heard.","steps":[{"t":"Say the core message","d":"One sentence the audience should still hold afterwards; everything else serves it."},{"t":"Choose three to five points","d":"Fewer, deeper points survive; a long list is a document, not a talk."},{"t":"One idea per slide","d":"The slide shows the evidence for that idea, not the sentence you intend to say."},{"t":"Signpost the joins","d":"Say what just happened and what comes next, so the room never has to guess."},{"t":"Rehearse out loud","d":"Standing, timed, including the opening and every transition between sections."},{"t":"Close with an ask","d":"Repeat the message once and state what the audience should do now."}],"oneLiner":"One idea per slide, signposted joins, rehearsed to time — an audience can hold one thing at a time.","exam":"Answer in three blocks — planning, organizing, delivering — naming the introduction-body-conclusion shapes. Put the 6x6 rule and one-idea-per-slide under composing, and eye contact, pace and handling questions under presenting. Naming the pattern chosen, such as problem-solution or chronological, earns the extra marks."},{"n":10,"name":"Recommendation Reports","hook":"The ward must choose one of three sites for a water tank, and the council meets on Friday.","why":"The person who decides was not at the site. If the report only says what the writer saw, the council either re-walks all three sites or picks by whoever spoke loudest. And if the writer scores the sites before agreeing what 'better' means, every option looks defensible and the meeting argues about preferences instead of evidence.","story":"Three sites for a ward water tank. The engineer walks all three, then does something that feels backwards: before scoring anything she writes down how the ward will judge them — land title, distance to the main pipe, cost of the platform, access in monsoon. The council argues about those criteria once, in the abstract, and then cannot re-litigate them site by site. Only then does she fill the table, each option against each criterion, with the figure or the document behind it. Two sites fail on title; the third costs more but needs no land bought. She recommends that one, says what it trades away and what the ward does on Monday, because the council was not there and the report is the walk.","idea":"A recommendation report hands a decision to someone who was not present, so it must carry the reasoning in the order the reader will test it. Criteria come before options, because criteria chosen after seeing the options are only justification. Findings compare options against criteria with evidence, conclusions say what the comparison means, and recommendations say what to do, in what order, accepting which trade-off. Criteria written after the options are seen can only justify a preference already held, so they stop being evidence and become advocacy; fixing cost, time, risk and support first keeps the decision-maker arguing about the standard once instead of re-litigating each site.","steps":[{"t":"State problem and scope","d":"What decision is needed, by whom, and what this report will not cover."},{"t":"Fix criteria first","d":"The standards agreed before any option is scored or even named."},{"t":"Compare with evidence","d":"Options against criteria in one table, each cell traceable to a source."},{"t":"Conclude from the table","d":"What the comparison shows, without yet saying what anyone should do."},{"t":"Recommend one course","d":"Specific, ordered, costed, with the trade-off named out loud."}],"oneLiner":"Criteria before options, evidence before opinion, and one recommendation the reader can act on without you.","exam":"Define it and separate it from the information report in one line. Then list the structure in order — problem, criteria, findings, conclusions, recommendation — and say why criteria must precede options. A small options-by-criteria table drawn inside the answer usually earns more than another paragraph of prose."},{"n":11,"name":"Proposals","hook":"A training institute promises a bank three days of Excel training, and names the price before being asked.","why":"A proposal that describes good work but never says what will be delivered, by when, by whom and for how much leaves the reader unable to say yes. They must write back with questions, the decision slips a month, and the contract goes to whoever answered in a form that could be signed. Enthusiasm is not a proposal.","story":"The bank's HR office sends out a request: three days of Excel training for forty staff, before the audit. The institute that wins does not send a brochure about how good it is. It says what each of the three days contains, who teaches it, what the staff can do on the fourth day, what the bank must provide, when the invoice comes, and what happens to the fee if the bank postpones. It also says what it will not do. Where a recommendation report tells the bank what to decide, this document tells the bank what the institute will do for it, and the price is not an embarrassment at the end but a line in the plan.","idea":"A proposal is a promise with a price, written so the reader can say yes without a meeting. A recommendation report only tells the reader what to decide; accepting it commissions nobody, while accepting a proposal commissions the writer. That means the problem in the reader's own words, the work described concretely enough to be checked, a schedule, named people, an itemized budget with its assumptions, and acceptance criteria that define finished. Persuasion comes from evidence and a realistic price, not from adjectives.","steps":[{"t":"Restate their problem","d":"In the reader's words, so they recognise the document as being about them."},{"t":"Promise specific work","d":"Deliverables and method, concrete enough that 'done' is not arguable later."},{"t":"Show the schedule","d":"When each part happens, and what the client must supply for it to happen."},{"t":"Price it openly","d":"Itemized budget with assumptions, so the total can be tested rather than just accepted."},{"t":"Name the acceptance","d":"How both sides will agree, in advance, that the work is finished."}],"oneLiner":"A proposal is a promise with a price: this work, by these people, by this date, for this sum.","exam":"Define it, then sort the types — solicited or unsolicited, internal or external — in a two-line grid. Give the informal shape in five lines and the formal structure in order from transmittal letter to budget. The difference from a recommendation report is a reliable short-answer mark."},{"n":12,"name":"Ethics in the Workplace","hook":"The Dashain bonus memo is due at five, and three contract staff are not on the list.","why":"A document can be accurate line by line and still mislead. Leave out the three names, start the axis at ninety per cent, put the good month in the headline and the bad month in an appendix, and every sentence survives checking while the reader is deceived. The writer's signature is on it, and 'the figures were correct' is not a defence anyone accepts.","story":"The Dashain bonus list. The branch has twenty-two staff and a budget for nineteen, because three are on contract. The memo can say 'all staff received the festival allowance' and be technically true of the permanent staff. It can also carry a bar chart whose axis starts at ninety, so a small drop looks like a collapse. Nobody asks the writer to lie; they ask her to choose a wording, a scale and a cut-off date under deadline pressure, with a promotion in the room. Ethics is that row of small choices, and the memo that survives is the one where the three names appear, the axis starts at zero, and the reason is on the page.","idea":"Ethics is what you do where the rulebook stops and someone still has to choose. Accuracy is a property of each sentence, while honesty is a property of the whole document: omitting three contract staff from a bonus memo, starting a chart axis at ninety per cent, or moving a bad month to an appendix leaves every figure correct yet steers the reader to a false conclusion. The test is whether a reader holding only this document would reach the conclusion the writer reached.","steps":[{"t":"Name the dilemma","d":"Say what is being asked and who is affected, before choosing any wording."},{"t":"Gather the facts","d":"What is actually true, what is unknown, and what you are quietly assuming."},{"t":"List the stakeholders","d":"Who gains, who is protected, and who is not in the room to object."},{"t":"Test against the reader","d":"Would a reader with only this document reach the same conclusion you did?"},{"t":"Decide, record, review","d":"Write the reason down, act on it, and check the outcome afterwards."}],"oneLiner":"Ethics in writing is whether a reader with only your document would reach the same conclusion you did.","exam":"Define ethics as judgement where rules run out, then list the core values with one line each. Structure the dilemma answer as the six numbered steps — that sequence is where the marks sit. The writing-specific part, fabricated data, truncated axes, undisclosed sources and plagiarism, is where examples earn the rest."}],"ecom":[{"n":1,"name":"Introduction","hook":"A kirana shop in Asan needs no website; take away its four walls and it needs a law, a courier and a bank.","why":"A shop works because everything it needs is within walking distance: the counter, the ward office, the supplier, the customer's face. Move the sale onto a network and each of those becomes a stranger with its own rules, and the order completes only if all of them cooperate. Nothing in the technology forces cooperation, so the gaps surface as abandoned carts and buyers who trust only cash.","story":"Take the kirana shop on the corner of Asan and put it on a web page. The shopkeeper believes he has removed four walls and a rent bill. What he has really done is pull the building apart and hand each part to a stranger. The counter boy who watched the till becomes a payment gateway; the ward licence and the consumer law become a policy layer he no longer controls; the painted signboard becomes a catalogue plus an advertising budget; the padlock, the delivery boy and the cash box become support services; and the Tuesday supplier becomes a business partnership. He still needs all five, and the weakest one now sets the speed of the business, which is why cash on delivery wins wherever the payment layer is thin.","idea":"E-commerce is the same shop with its walls replaced by five layers that must all work: people, public policy, marketing, support services and partnerships. A website only publishes an offer, while completing a sale still needs people to staff it, law to enforce the record, marketing to be found, support services to move money and goods, and partners to supply the stock. Remove any layer and the order dies at that point, which is why cash on delivery dominates wherever the payment layer is thin.","steps":[{"t":"Sell across a network","d":"Buying and selling moves onto TCP/IP, so the seller needs no shopfront and the buyer needs no travel."},{"t":"Notice distance stops costing","d":"One page serves Kathmandu and Kansas at the same marginal cost; that is what ubiquity and global reach mean in the accounts."},{"t":"Watch prices become visible","d":"Information density shows price, cost and reviews to every buyer at once, so margin survives only on service, selection or speed."},{"t":"Name who sells to whom","d":"B2C, B2B, C2C, C2B, m-commerce and u-commerce are the same transaction with the seller, the buyer and the device written out."},{"t":"Check the five layers","d":"People staff the sale, public policy enforces the record, marketing makes the shop findable, support services move money and goods, and partners supply the stock. They each fail independently, and the order dies at the first failure."}],"oneLiner":"E-commerce removes the shop's walls and replaces them with five layers that must all hold at once.","exam":"For a ten-mark definition, give e-commerce against e-business in one line, then draw the five layers and say what breaks if each is missing. Add pure versus partial with an e-book and a Daraz delivery, and close with one Nepali constraint such as cash on delivery."},{"n":2,"name":"E-commerce Business Model","hook":"The shop sells one egg at a time until a restaurant asks for two hundred, invoiced at month end.","why":"A shop that cannot say in one sentence who pays it, for what, and why that customer returns is a hobby with stock. The failure is quiet: the site launches, orders arrive, and the owner discovers the price never covered delivery, or that the buyer came once for the discount. Without a written model every later decision on hiring, discounting and expansion is taken blind.","story":"The kirana shop sells one egg at a time, and the shopkeeper can state his business in a sentence: they pay cash, I hand over the egg, they come back because I am open at nine at night. Then the restaurants on the same street ask for two hundred eggs at six each morning, invoiced at month end. Same eggs, same shop, but the sentence has changed, because who pays, when, and why they stay have all moved. He now needs a dawn delivery route, a credit book, and an order that arrives as a written list rather than a conversation. That list is EDI; the route is his competitive advantage. He has not added a product but a second business model, and the retail one will not run it.","idea":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom, for what, and why they keep paying. An e-tailer buys stock, holds it and keeps the margin between cost and price, so unsold inventory is its own loss; a market creator never owns the goods and charges sellers a fee or commission on completed trades, so its revenue follows volume while stock risk stays with the seller. That is why marketplaces grow on thin capital, and why the eight elements — value proposition, revenue model, market, rivals, advantage, strategy, organisation and team — decide whether the sentence survives the second year.","steps":[{"t":"Say why anyone buys","d":"The value proposition is the reason a buyer crosses the street; every other element exists to keep that reason true."},{"t":"Name how money arrives","d":"Sale, subscription, transaction fee, advertising, affiliate or freemium, chosen before costs are counted, not after."},{"t":"Let the payer shape it","d":"Consumers give the seven B2C models; firms give e-distributors, e-procurement, exchanges and private networks."},{"t":"Move documents without people","d":"An EDI translator maps the order into a standard, a network carries it, the partner's translator maps it back."},{"t":"Find where margin settles","d":"The industry chain and the firm's own chain show which activity keeps the difference between cost and price."}],"oneLiner":"A business model is one sentence about who pays whom and why they keep paying; the eight elements make it true.","exam":"Open with the sentence itself, not the definition list. Then take the eight elements in order from value proposition to management team, showing each supporting that sentence. Change the payer from consumer to firm for B2C against B2B, and finish with EDI as the document layer underneath."},{"n":3,"name":"Electronic Payment System","hook":"At a Daraz checkout the card number crosses six hands, and every one of them could copy it.","why":"A card number is the account itself, so anyone who reads it can spend from it and the buyer cannot tell who copied it. Encrypting the wire fixes the middle and leaves both ends open: the shop must read the order, the bank must read the account, and each ends up holding what it has no business holding. SET exists to close exactly that gap.","story":"You pay a shop in another city by sending a sealed parcel on the night bus. The conductor sees only the outside, which carries nothing but an address. Inside are two covers: an outer one addressed to the shop, holding your order, and a thin inner envelope addressed to the bank, holding the card number. The shop opens its cover, reads what you ordered, and passes the inner envelope on without being able to open it. The bank opens that one and never learns what you bought. One wax seal runs across both covers, so neither side can slide a different order or amount underneath, and both can prove the halves left your hand together. That seal is the dual signature.","idea":"An e-payment moves value through a chain: cardholder, merchant, gateway, acquiring bank, association, issuing bank. SET splits the purchase into order information for the shop and payment information for the bank, and the cardholder signs the two digests together, so nobody on the chain sees the whole purchase and nobody can repudiate their half. The bank must read the card and the merchant must read the order, yet neither may read both, so altering either half breaks verification and no party can deny the half it sent.","steps":[{"t":"Both parties register","d":"Cardholder registers through the issuer and merchant through the acquirer; each receives an X.509 certificate from a certificate authority."},{"t":"Split the purchase","d":"Order information and payment information are hashed separately and never travel as one readable document."},{"t":"Seal both halves","d":"The cardholder signs the concatenated digests, binding order and payment with a single verifiable signature. One signature over the whole purchase would hand the merchant the card data and the bank the order, so each half is hashed and only the two digests are signed together. Because the one signature covers both digests, altering either half breaks verification, and the cardholder cannot deny the half he sent."},{"t":"Forward the envelope blind","d":"The merchant reads the order, then passes the payment half to the gateway without the ability to open it."},{"t":"Authorise, then capture","d":"The gateway checks the issuer for a hold; capture turns that hold into a charge, and settlement moves the money."}],"oneLiner":"SET is a double envelope: the shop reads the order, the bank reads the card, one seal proves they belong together.","exam":"Draw the six participants first, then walk one purchase request through them in order. Name the dual signature and say what each party can and cannot read, which is where the extra marks sit, then one line on why TLS and 3-D Secure displaced it."},{"n":4,"name":"Building E-commerce System","hook":"In a big shop the boy at the counter keeps your pile; online, every click hires a new boy.","why":"A web page forgets everything between clicks, because HTTP carries no memory from one request to the next. Without somewhere to write the basket down, a second item empties the first, and a refresh loses the lot. If the anonymous basket is not merged at login, the site deletes the work it has just watched a customer do, and the loss never appears in the logs.","story":"In a big shop in New Road you do not carry your goods. You hand them to the boy at the counter, he keeps a pile with your name on it, and you keep shopping. If the shop kept no slip, two things would go wrong the same afternoon: your pile would be handed to somebody else, and at closing nobody could say what you had taken. A website is that shop with no memory between pages. So the slip has to be written outside the page, in a server session or in the browser, and it has to survive the moment a stranger becomes a named customer at login. The slip is still not a purchase; only the bill at the counter turns a pile into a sale.","idea":"A shopping cart is a claim slip written outside the page, so the basket survives navigation, refresh and login. Checkout freezes the slip into an order with a fixed total and a legal sequence of states. The browser's return page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or abandon the tab before it loads; payment is proved by a server-to-server callback whose signature the store verifies and applies once through an idempotency key. A shop trusting the redirect ships goods nobody paid for.","steps":[{"t":"Make the catalogue data","d":"Products, variants, price and stock live in one database; hand-coded pages cannot keep three copies of a price correct."},{"t":"Write the claim down","d":"Add, update and remove items in a server session or browser store; nothing is bought and no stock is reserved yet."},{"t":"Merge on login","d":"The anonymous slip is combined with the saved cart, so nothing the customer chose disappears at the door."},{"t":"Freeze into an order","d":"Tax and shipping are computed once, and the order moves placed, paid, packed, shipped, delivered in that order only."},{"t":"Let the callback decide","d":"An idempotency key and a verified webhook mark it paid. That return page is only a URL the browser was told to open, so a shopper can type it, replay it, or close the tab before it loads; only a callback the shop's own server receives and verifies is evidence."}],"oneLiner":"A cart is a claim slip outside the page; checkout turns it into an order only a verified callback can mark paid.","exam":"Answer by which promise each component keeps: catalogue, cart, checkout, payment callback, back office. The ten-mark version names the order state machine, ACID on the payment write, and why the return URL is not proof of payment."},{"n":5,"name":"Security in E-Commerce","hook":"Six things can go wrong with one deposit, and a camera only stops one of them.","why":"The word secure hides six different failures. A shop can encrypt the wire and still let a clerk rewrite the amount, lose the ledger, or deny having signed it, and each of those needs a different mechanism. Because the word is never split, everyone reaches for encryption as a universal answer and the audit later finds five doors nobody locked.","story":"The sahakari on the corner keeps its members' savings in one room, and the money passes through four hands: the member, the counter clerk, the ledger keeper and the bank. Each hand is a different place for a different thing to go wrong. A stranger can claim to be a member; a clerk can raise a figure by ten; a courier can read the withdrawal slip; a fire can take the ledger; a signer can later swear the entry is not his; and the ledger can sit open where the whole neighbourhood reads who defaulted. Six harms, six locks. A camera does nothing for the page somebody rewrote, and a signature does nothing for the fire, which is why the locks are chosen harm by harm.","idea":"Security is a chain of custody judged on six properties: confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation and privacy. Each property names a specific harm and each harm names a mechanism, so the chain holds only where every link has its own lock. A hash is a one-way digest, so altering a single digit of the amount produces a completely different value and tampering becomes visible; but anyone can compute that same digest, so it says nothing about who wrote the message. Only encrypting the digest with the sender's private key, verified through a certificate, adds authorship and non-repudiation.","steps":[{"t":"Name the six properties","d":"Confidentiality, integrity, availability, authenticity, non-repudiation and privacy each answer a different harm."},{"t":"Match threat to door","d":"Malicious code and phishing work the client, sniffing and pharming the wire, injection and insiders the server."},{"t":"Hash for integrity","d":"A one-way digest of fixed length changes completely if a single digit of the amount changes, so tampering becomes visible. But anyone can compute that same digest, so on its own it says nothing about who wrote the message."},{"t":"Sign the hash for proof","d":"The sender's private key over that digest gives authenticity and non-repudiation in one step."},{"t":"Bind key to a name","d":"An X.509 certificate signed by a certificate authority turns a key into an identity other parties can trust."},{"t":"Let law enforce it","d":"The Electronic Transactions Act gives records and signatures legal force and makes unauthorised access an offence."}],"oneLiner":"Security is a chain of custody with six properties, and it breaks at the one link you did not lock.","exam":"List the six dimensions, then give each one threat and one countermeasure so the examiner sees a mapping and not a recitation. Close with hash to signature to certificate as a single chain of trust, and the Act as the legal teeth."},{"n":6,"name":"Digital Marketing","hook":"The hoarding above the paan shop is rented by the month and nobody knows who it brings in.","why":"Advertising was a guess bought in bulk: half wasted, with no way to say which half. Digital channels do not remove the guess, they price it, because every impression, click and sale can be counted. The trap is that the same numbers flatter at the top of the funnel and punish at the bottom, so a campaign judged by clicks alone looks healthy while losing money per order.","story":"The shopkeeper on New Road rents the hoarding above the paan shop by the month and has no idea how many passers-by ever walk in. Online, the same hoarding is rented by the thousand views, which is CPM, and the landlord can instead charge only when somebody walks in, which is CPC, or only when somebody buys, which is CPA. Each contract simply moves the risk of a wasted view between advertiser and publisher, and that is all a pricing model has ever been. The rent is no longer negotiated once a year either: the space is auctioned per impression, in the milliseconds before the page finishes loading, to whoever values that particular pair of eyes most.","idea":"Digital marketing rents attention by the unit that can be counted and lets an auction set the price for each unit. Targeting decides whose attention is worth bidding for, the exchange sells it impression by impression, and acquisition cost measured against lifetime value decides whether the whole exercise made money. Click-through rate says only whether the creative earned attention, never whether the visit returned anything, so a channel whose acquisition cost exceeds lifetime value loses money on every sale however healthy its click rate looks.","steps":[{"t":"Choose the audience","d":"Demographic, geographic, contextual, behavioural, lookalike and retargeting segments, all built from cookies and device identifiers."},{"t":"Auction the impression","d":"The supply-side platform offers the slot, demand-side platforms bid, and real-time bidding closes before the page renders."},{"t":"Fix the contract","d":"CPM pays for attention, CPC for interest, CPA or CPL for action, CPV for a view, flat fee for certainty."},{"t":"Measure the funnel","d":"Impressions and reach for exposure, click-through rate for the creative, conversion rate and acquisition cost for efficiency."},{"t":"Compare with lifetime value","d":"A channel costing more than the customer returns is a leak, however good its click-through rate looks."}],"oneLiner":"Digital marketing rents attention by the countable unit, at a price an auction sets in milliseconds.","exam":"Define the channels in one line, then spend the marks on pricing models with a worked example: CPM for reach, CPC for interest, CPA for action. Name DSP, SSP and real-time bidding, and close by judging the result on acquisition cost against lifetime value."},{"n":7,"name":"Optimizing E-commerce Systems","hook":"A librarian who has read everything decides which shelf you meet first, and notices what you take next.","why":"A shop that is perfect and invisible earns nothing, and a shop that is found but suggests nothing sells one item per visit. Both losses come from the same missing step: the site either was never read by the machine that ranks it, or it knows what the visitor wants and never uses it. Without that work, traffic is bought again every month and returning visitors are strangers again.","story":"Think of the librarian at a busy pustakalaya who has read everything and remembers who borrows what. Ask for a book on accounting and she does not walk the shelves at random; she goes to the titles other books point to, because the book that many respected books cite sits nearest the door. That is PageRank: a citation is a vote, and a vote from a well-cited book outweighs one from a pamphlet nobody opens. She also reads the borrowers. When the last ten people who took the auditing book also took the taxation one, she puts that in your hands without having read either. That is collaborative filtering, and both jobs are one job: raising the chance this visitor leaves with something.","idea":"Optimisation raises the odds that a given visitor buys, once before arrival by being crawled, indexed and ranked, and once after arrival by being shown the next plausible item. Collaborative filtering needs a user-item matrix, so a visitor with no history has no neighbours to borrow from, and a new item nobody bought has none either; content-based filtering compares item features with what that visitor already viewed, so it recommends before any community behaviour exists. Hybrid systems fall back on popularity or attributes until history accumulates, and both halves are measured, never assumed.","steps":[{"t":"Be reachable at all","d":"Crawlable links, a sitemap and a sane robots file; a blocked page never enters the inverted index."},{"t":"Make the page legible","d":"One clear heading, keyword in title and URL, internal links, alt text, structured data, fast mobile rendering."},{"t":"Earn citations","d":"Relevant inbound links with honest anchor text; PageRank discounts the linker's weight and divides by its outbound count."},{"t":"Watch the funnel","d":"Analytics turns sessions into drop-off points: the product page, the cart, or the payment page."},{"t":"Suggest the next item","d":"Item-based filtering borrows other baskets, content-based matches features, production systems combine both."},{"t":"Fix the cold start","d":"A new user or item has no history, so fall back to popularity, attributes or context until behaviour arrives."}],"oneLiner":"SEO convinces the librarian to shelve you near the door; recommendation notices what other borrowers took with your book.","exam":"Split the answer at the moment of arrival. Before: crawl, index, rank, with on-page, off-page and technical beneath it, and PageRank written as the formula. After: collaborative against content-based, with cold start as the honest limitation and one named metric as the closing mark."}]};
